import{t as e}from"./links-ClIw6rLF.js";var t={id:`S2`,title:`Solar PV and Hybrid Engineering`,level:`advanced`,stage:`specialist`,summary:`Photovoltaic fundamentals and module datasheets; strings, MPPT and PWM controllers and solar inverters; complete system design from an actual load profile (inverter, battery, array, controller, cables and protection); off-grid, grid-tied and hybrid systems with generator integration and energy management; installation design, commissioning and troubleshooting.`,prerequisites:[`F1`,`F2`],recommended:[`S1`,`P5`],status:`published`,phase:7,competency:`CU-12`,modules:[{id:`S2.M1`,title:`Photovoltaic fundamentals`,summary:`How solar cells convert light to electricity, how modules are built and specified, and how to calculate their voltage, current and power under real irradiance and temperature.`,lessons:[{id:`S2.M1.L1`,title:`Solar photovoltaic fundamentals`,minutes:40,content:{objectives:[`Explain the photovoltaic effect in a silicon p–n junction.`,`Describe a PV cell with the single-diode model.`,`Interpret I–V and P–V curves and identify V_oc, I_sc and the maximum power point.`,`Explain why a PV module behaves as a current source over most of its curve.`],introduction:`A solar cell converts light directly into electricity with no moving parts. Every design decision in this course — how many modules in a string, which controller, how big the cables — follows from the shape of one curve: the cell's current–voltage characteristic. This lesson explains where that curve comes from. The [[pv|PV]] I–V laboratory lets you explore it.`,lecture:`### The photovoltaic effect

A silicon solar cell is a large, thin [[diode]]:

- a thin **n-type** layer (doped to have free electrons) on top of a thicker **p-type** wafer (doped to have "holes");
- at the junction between them, an internal electric field forms.

When a photon with enough energy is absorbed, it frees an electron and leaves a hole. The junction field pushes electrons towards the n side and holes towards the p side. Connect an external circuit and this separated charge flows: a **light-generated current**, proportional to the light absorbed.

### The single-diode model

A cell behaves like:

- a **current source I_ph** proportional to [[irradiance]];
- in parallel with a **diode** (the junction itself), which takes some of that current as the voltage rises;
- with a small **series resistance R_s** (fingers, busbars, contacts).

**I = I_ph − I₀ · [e^((V + I·R_s)/a) − 1]**

where I₀ is the diode's saturation current and a = n·N_cells·kT/q depends on the number of cells in series and the temperature. The laboratory fits I₀, a and R_s to each module's datasheet.

### The I–V curve

- **Short-circuit current [[isc|I_sc]]** (V = 0): all the light current flows out. It is proportional to irradiance.
- **Open-circuit voltage [[voc|V_oc]]** (I = 0): all the light current flows through the internal diode. For one silicon cell it is about 0.6–0.7 V; a module adds cells in series.
- Between them, the curve is nearly flat (a **current source**) until the diode starts conducting near V_oc, where it falls steeply (a **voltage source**).

### The power curve and the maximum power point

Power P = V·I is zero at both ends of the curve and has a maximum at the **maximum power point (MPP)**: V_mp, I_mp, P_max. Typically:

- V_mp ≈ 0.8 × V_oc;
- I_mp ≈ 0.93 × I_sc for modern modules.

Operating anywhere else wastes power. Keeping the array at its MPP is the job of an [[mppt|MPPT]] controller (lesson S2.M2.L2).

### Why it matters for safety

A PV module is a current source whenever it is lit:

- it **cannot be switched off** at the module;
- short-circuiting it does not produce a huge current (only I_sc), so **fuses barely respond** to many PV faults;
- strings of modules produce hazardous DC voltages in daylight (F1.M2.L3).`,technical:`**Fill factor.** FF = P_max / (V_oc · I_sc) measures how "square" the curve is: about 0.75–0.82 for good crystalline modules. Series resistance and shunt leakage reduce it. For the example 400 W module (illustrative values V_oc 37.1 V, I_sc 13.8 A): FF = 400 / (37.1 × 13.8) = 0.781.

**Efficiency.** Module efficiency = P_max / (G × area). A 400 W module of 1.95 m² at 1000 W/m² is 400 / 1950 = 20.5 % efficient. Efficiency matters for roof area, not for the electrical design, which uses the module's ratings.

**Model limits.** The laboratory's single-diode model ignores shunt resistance and spectral effects. It reproduces the datasheet points and the trends with irradiance and temperature, which is what design teaching needs.`,diagrams:[{id:`pv-cell`,caption:`The photovoltaic effect in a p–n junction, and the single-diode equivalent circuit.`},{id:`iv-family`,caption:`I–V curves of the example module: current follows irradiance, voltage falls with temperature; dots mark the MPPs.`}],formulas:[{name:`Single-diode model`,expr:`I = I_ph − I₀ · [e^((V + I·R_s)/a) − 1]`,where:[[`I_ph`,`light-generated current (≈ I_sc)`,`A`],[`I₀`,`diode saturation current`,`A`],[`a`,`modified ideality factor n·N_cells·kT/q`,`V`],[`R_s`,`series resistance`,`Ω`]]},{name:`Fill factor`,expr:`FF = P_max / (V_oc · I_sc)`,where:[]},{name:`Module efficiency`,expr:`η = P_max / (G · A)`,where:[[`G`,`irradiance`,`W/m²`],[`A`,`module area`,`m²`]]}],examples:[{title:`Fill factor and efficiency`,problem:`A module has V_oc = 49.6 V, I_sc = 14.0 A, P_max = 550 W and an area of 2.58 m² (illustrative values). Find its fill factor and efficiency.`,steps:[`FF = 550 / (49.6 × 14.0) = 550 / 694.4 = 0.792`,`η = 550 / (1000 × 2.58) = 0.213 = 21.3 %`],answer:`FF = 0.79; efficiency 21.3 %.`},{title:`Where on the curve?`,problem:`A module (V_oc 37.1 V, V_mp 31.0 V, I_mp 12.9 A, I_sc 13.8 A) is connected straight across a 12.8 V battery. Roughly what current and power does it deliver?`,steps:[`12.8 V is far below V_mp, on the flat (current-source) part of the curve, so I ≈ I_sc ≈ 13.5 A`,`P ≈ 12.8 × 13.5 ≈ 173 W, compared with P_max = 400 W`],answer:`About 13.5 A and 173 W: less than half the module’s rating. This is why MPPT matters (S2.M2.L2).`}],applications:`Every PV system design, MPPT algorithm, fault-finding measurement (V_oc and I_sc tests) and safety rule for PV work starts from this curve. The PV I–V laboratory traces it for any module, irradiance and temperature.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Treating a PV module like a battery (a voltage source).`,correction:`Over most of its curve it is a current source set by irradiance; its voltage depends on the load and temperature.`},{mistake:`Assuming a short circuit on a PV string will blow a fuse.`,correction:`Short-circuit current is only about I_sc; protection must be designed for that.`},{mistake:`Thinking a module can be switched off.`,correction:`It generates whenever lit; isolate with DC isolators and treat conductors as live.`}],demo:{title:`I–V and P–V curves`,text:`Change irradiance and cell temperature and watch V_oc, I_sc and the maximum power point move on the curves computed by the single-diode model.`,link:e.solar(`Solar laboratory: PV I–V tracer`,`iv`),embed:`pv-iv`},activities:[e.solar(`PV I–V laboratory`,`iv`),e.lib(`Solar input`,`solar-input`),e.proc(`PV open-circuit voltage test`,`solar-input`,`pv-voc`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`What separates the electron–hole pairs created by light in a solar cell?`,options:[`The electric field at the p–n junction`,`The glass cover`,`The series resistance`,`The bypass diode`],answer:0,explain:`The junction field drives electrons and holes apart.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`I_sc of a module is proportional to…`,options:[`irradiance`,`temperature only`,`the battery voltage`,`the cable length`],answer:0,explain:`The light current scales with irradiance.`},{id:`k3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Fill factor of a module with V_oc 46 V, I_sc 9.3 A, P_max 330 W?`,answer:.7714,tol:.01,unit:``,explain:`330/(46 × 9.3) = 0.771.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`On the flat part of the I–V curve a module behaves as…`,options:[`a current source`,`a voltage source`,`a short circuit`,`an open circuit`],answer:0,explain:`Current is almost constant as voltage changes.`}],summary:[`Light frees electron–hole pairs; the junction field separates them into a current.`,`Single-diode model: current source in parallel with a diode, with series resistance.`,`I_sc ∝ irradiance; V_oc ≈ 0.6–0.7 V per silicon cell.`,`Power peaks at the MPP (V_mp ≈ 0.8 V_oc); operating elsewhere wastes power.`,`PV is live whenever lit and limited to about I_sc in a fault.`],homework:{id:`S2.M1.L1.hw`,title:`Reading the curve`,brief:`Illustrative module: P_max 100 W, V_oc 22.0 V, I_sc 5.9 A, V_mp 18.0 V, I_mp 5.56 A, area 0.67 m².`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Fill factor?`,answer:.7704,tol:.01,unit:``,explain:`100/(22 × 5.9) = 0.770.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Efficiency (%)?`,answer:14.93,tol:.01,unit:`%`,explain:`100/(1000 × 0.67) = 14.9 %.`},{id:`h3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`V_mp/V_oc ratio?`,answer:.818,tol:.01,unit:``,explain:`18/22.`}],written:[{prompt:`Explain, using the I–V curve, why a fault on a PV string may not blow a fuse, and what this means for safety.`,model:`A short circuit moves the operating point to I_sc, only slightly above normal operating current, so a fuse sized above normal current may never operate. PV faults can therefore persist and arc while the sun shines; designers rely on correct string protection, DC isolators, insulation monitoring and safe isolation rather than expecting fuses to clear every fault.`}]}}},{id:`S2.M1.L2`,title:`Solar panel construction and specifications`,minutes:35,content:{objectives:[`Describe the construction of a crystalline-silicon PV module.`,`Read a module datasheet: STC ratings, NOCT, temperature coefficients, tolerances and limits.`,`Distinguish manufacturer values, typical values and simulated values.`,`Identify the datasheet limits that constrain system design (maximum system voltage, maximum series fuse).`],introduction:`A module datasheet is the starting point of every PV calculation. Reading it correctly — and knowing which numbers are guaranteed limits and which are typical — is a professional skill. This lesson takes a module apart, then reads its datasheet line by line. The modules in this programme's laboratory carry *illustrative* values, clearly labelled; a real design always uses the chosen product's datasheet.`,lecture:`### Construction

From front to back, a typical crystalline module has:

1. **tempered glass** (about 3.2 mm), anti-reflective coated;
2. an **encapsulant** layer (EVA or POE) bonding everything together;
3. the **cells**: 60/72 full cells or 108/120/144 half-cells, connected in series by ribbons or wires;
4. a second encapsulant layer;
5. a **backsheet** (polymer) or a second glass (glass–glass and bifacial modules);
6. an **aluminium frame** for strength and mounting, bonded to earth in many installations;
7. a **junction box** with the **[[bypass-diode|bypass diodes]]** (usually three) and two output cables with polarised DC connectors.

Half-cell modules cut each cell in two, halving the current in each half and reducing resistive losses; they are wired as two parallel halves.

### Standard Test Conditions

Ratings are measured at **[[stc|STC]]**: 1000 W/m², 25 °C cell temperature, air mass 1.5 spectrum. Real conditions are almost never STC: cells usually run much hotter.

### The electrical table (STC)

| Item | Meaning |
|---|---|
| P_max | Maximum power (W), with a power tolerance such as 0/+5 W |
| V_mp, I_mp | Voltage and current at maximum power |
| V_oc | Open-circuit voltage |
| I_sc | Short-circuit current |
| Module efficiency | P_max / (1000 W/m² × area) |

### Temperature behaviour

- **[[noct|NOCT]]** (or NMOT): the cell temperature in standard field-like conditions, typically about 42–46 °C.
- **[[temp-coefficient|Temperature coefficients]]**:
  - of P_max, γ (about −0.3 to −0.4 %/°C);
  - of V_oc, β (about −0.25 to −0.35 %/°C);
  - of I_sc, α (about +0.04 to +0.06 %/°C).

### Limits that constrain the design

- **Maximum system voltage** (for example 1000 V or 1500 V DC): the string's cold V_oc must never exceed it.
- **Maximum series fuse rating** (for example 25 A): the largest reverse current the module may carry; it decides whether [[string-fuse|string fuses]] are needed.
- **Operating temperature range**, mechanical load ratings, fire and safety class.

### Three kinds of value

- **Manufacturer (datasheet) values:** measured and guaranteed within stated tolerances for that product. These go into real designs.
- **Typical values:** representative of a class of products (as in "β is about −0.3 %/°C"). Useful for learning and first estimates, never for final design.
- **Simulated values:** produced by a model, such as the laboratory's I–V tracer. They show behaviour, not guaranteed performance.`,technical:`**NOCT and cell temperature.** A common estimate is T_cell = T_ambient + (NOCT − 20)/800 × G. For NOCT 45 °C at 35 °C ambient and 800 W/m²: T_cell = 35 + 25 = 60 °C. Mounting matters: modules flush on a roof run hotter than open-rack ones.

**Power tolerance and degradation.** A positive-only tolerance (0/+5 W) means no module is below its rating at delivery. Output then degrades slowly (often warranted to stay above a stated fraction after 25 years); designs may allow for it.

**Bifacial modules.** These also collect reflected light from the rear, so their effective output depends on the ground. Datasheets give a "bifaciality" factor and ratings at several rear-irradiance levels.`,diagrams:[{id:`pv-cell`,caption:`Cells in series behind glass; bypass diodes in the junction box protect groups of cells.`}],formulas:[{name:`Cell temperature (NOCT method)`,expr:`T_cell = T_amb + (NOCT − 20) / 800 · G`,where:[[`NOCT`,`nominal operating cell temperature`,`°C`],[`G`,`irradiance`,`W/m²`]]},{name:`Temperature correction`,expr:`X(T) = X_STC · [1 + c · (T_cell − 25)]`,where:[[`c`,`temperature coefficient of X`,`%/°C ÷ 100`]]}],examples:[{title:`Hot module power`,problem:`An illustrative 400 W module has γ = −0.35 %/°C and NOCT 45 °C. Estimate its P_max at 1000 W/m² with the cells at 60 °C.`,steps:[`ΔT = 60 − 25 = 35 °C`,`P = 400 × (1 − 0.0035 × 35) = 400 × 0.8775 = 351 W`],answer:`About 351 W: a 12 % loss from heat alone.`},{title:`Cell temperature`,problem:`Find the cell temperature of a module with NOCT 44 °C on a 30 °C day at 900 W/m².`,steps:[`T_cell = 30 + (44 − 20)/800 × 900`,`T_cell = 30 + 0.03 × 900 = 30 + 27 = 57 °C`],answer:`57 °C.`}],applications:`Datasheet values feed every step of the S2.M3 sizing method and the S2.M5 commissioning tests. The laboratory lets you replace the illustrative module with values from a real datasheet.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Using STC power as the real output.`,correction:`Correct for temperature, irradiance, soiling and wiring losses.`},{mistake:`Ignoring the maximum system voltage and maximum series fuse rating.`,correction:`They are hard limits that constrain string length and protection.`},{mistake:`Treating typical or simulated values as datasheet values.`,correction:`Final designs use the manufacturer’s data for the chosen product.`}],demo:{title:`Enter a datasheet`,text:`On the solar laboratory’s Design tab, select an example module and then edit any field to enter a real datasheet; the I–V tracer and the sizing use your values.`,link:e.solar(`Solar laboratory: module datasheet fields`,`design`),embed:`pv-iv`},activities:[e.solar(`PV I–V laboratory`,`iv`),e.lib(`Solar input`,`solar-input`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`STC means…`,options:[`1000 W/m², 25 °C cell, AM1.5`,`800 W/m², 20 °C ambient`,`the hottest summer day`,`the module’s maximum voltage`],answer:0,explain:`Standard Test Conditions.`},{id:`k2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Cell temperature: NOCT 45 °C, ambient 25 °C, 1000 W/m² (°C)?`,answer:56.25,tol:.01,unit:`°C`,explain:`25 + 25/800 × 1000 = 56.25 °C.`},{id:`k3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`P_max of a 550 W module (γ −0.35 %/°C) at 55 °C cell (W)?`,answer:492.25,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`550 × (1 − 0.0035 × 30) = 492.25 W.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which datasheet value decides whether string fuses are needed?`,options:[`Maximum series fuse rating`,`Module efficiency`,`Frame colour`,`NOCT`],answer:0,explain:`It is the largest reverse current the module may carry.`}],summary:[`Glass, encapsulant, series-connected cells, backsheet, frame and junction box with bypass diodes.`,`Ratings at STC; real cells run hotter (NOCT method).`,`Temperature coefficients: P_max and V_oc fall, I_sc rises slightly.`,`Maximum system voltage and maximum series fuse are design limits.`,`Manufacturer, typical and simulated values are different things.`],homework:{id:`S2.M1.L2.hw`,title:`Datasheet reading`,brief:`Illustrative module: P_max 330 W, V_oc 46.0 V, I_sc 9.3 A, γ −0.39 %/°C, β −0.31 %/°C, α +0.05 %/°C, NOCT 45 °C.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Cell temperature at 32 °C ambient, 800 W/m² (°C)?`,answer:57,tol:.01,unit:`°C`,explain:`32 + 25 = 57 °C.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`P_max at that cell temperature and 1000 W/m² (W)?`,answer:288.8,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`330 × (1 − 0.0039 × 32) = 330 × 0.8752 = 288.8 W.`},{id:`h3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`V_oc at 57 °C (V)?`,answer:41.44,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`46 × (1 − 0.0031 × 32) = 46 × 0.9008 = 41.44 V.`}],written:[{prompt:`Explain the difference between a datasheet value, a typical value and a simulated value, with an example of each from this lesson.`,model:`Datasheet: the chosen product’s measured, guaranteed ratings (e.g. this module’s V_oc 46.0 V at STC within tolerance). Typical: representative of a class (β about −0.3 %/°C for crystalline silicon). Simulated: model output (the I–V tracer’s curve at 600 W/m²). Only datasheet values are used for a final design.`}]}}},{id:`S2.M1.L3`,title:`PV voltage, current and power calculations`,minutes:40,content:{objectives:[`Calculate V_oc, I_sc and P_max at any irradiance and cell temperature.`,`Calculate the coldest-morning V_oc and hottest-day V_mp of a string.`,`Calculate daily energy from peak sun hours with derating factors.`,`Verify predictions with the laboratory’s I–V tracer.`],introduction:`System design needs three predictions: the **highest voltage** the array can ever produce (a cold, bright morning), the **lowest working voltage** (a hot afternoon), and the **energy** it delivers each day. This lesson turns the datasheet into those numbers. The laboratory's measurement task asks you to make the same predictions and checks them against the model.`,lecture:`### Current with irradiance and temperature

**I_sc(G, T) = I_sc,STC · (G / 1000) · [1 + α(T − 25)]**

Current is directly proportional to irradiance and rises slightly with temperature.

### Voltage with temperature

**V_oc(T) = V_oc,STC · [1 + β(T − 25)]**

β is negative, so voltage **rises in the cold**. The highest voltage occurs on the coldest morning, when the cells are at about the ambient temperature and the sun is already bright enough to bring them close to V_oc.

V_oc also falls slightly at low irradiance, logarithmically: by a·ln(G/1000), where a is the module's diode parameter (lesson L1). This is a small effect: the laboratory's task gives you a.

### Voltage at the maximum power point

V_mp falls with temperature at about the same rate as V_oc:

**V_mp,hot ≈ V_mp,STC · [1 + β(T_cell,hot − 25)]**

On a hot day the string's working voltage is lowest. It must remain above the minimum operating voltage of the MPPT controller or inverter.

### Power

**P(G, T) ≈ P_max,STC · (G / 1000) · [1 + γ(T − 25)]**

At low irradiance a small correction for the lower voltage improves the estimate (multiply by V_oc(G)/V_oc(1000)).

### Strings

For n modules in series:

- V_string = n × V_module;
- I_string = I_module;
- P_string = n × P_module.

Parallel strings add current (lesson S2.M2.L1).

### Energy per day

[[psh|Peak sun hours]] (PSH) express a day's solar energy as hours at 1000 W/m². An array of P_STC watts produces approximately:

**E_day = P_STC × PSH × k**

where k combines the losses:

- temperature: 1 + γ(T_cell − 25);
- soiling (typical 0.95–0.98);
- DC wiring (typical 0.97–0.99);
- module mismatch (typical 0.98).

Inverter or controller efficiency is applied after that.`,technical:`**Why not use NOCT power?** Datasheets often quote power at NOCT (800 W/m², ≈ 45 °C), which is closer to real conditions than STC. The sizing method in this course works from STC with explicit corrections, so each loss is visible and can be changed.

**Design temperatures.** The coldest expected ambient and hottest design ambient come from climate data for the site. Standards specify how they are chosen (for example the record or a statistical low). Using a too-mild cold temperature underestimates V_oc and is a common cause of damaged controllers.

**Checking with the tracer.** The I–V tracer solves the single-diode model directly; your linear estimates should agree to within a few per cent. Differences show the effect of the model's curvature at low irradiance.`,diagrams:[{id:`iv-family`,caption:`The same module at 1000 W/m² and 600 W/m² (25 °C) and at 65 °C: current scales with light, voltage falls with heat.`}],formulas:[{name:`Short-circuit current`,expr:`I_sc(G,T) = I_sc,STC · (G/1000) · [1 + α(T − 25)]`,where:[]},{name:`Open-circuit voltage`,expr:`V_oc(T) = V_oc,STC · [1 + β(T − 25)]  (+ a·ln(G/1000) at low light)`,where:[]},{name:`Power`,expr:`P ≈ P_STC · (G/1000) · [1 + γ(T − 25)]`,where:[]},{name:`Daily energy`,expr:`E_day = P_STC × PSH × k`,where:[[`PSH`,`peak sun hours`,`h`],[`k`,`combined derating factor`,`—`]]}],examples:[{title:`Coldest-morning string voltage`,problem:`A string of 4 illustrative 400 W modules (V_oc 37.1 V, β −0.27 %/°C) is installed where the coldest morning is −5 °C. What is the highest string voltage?`,steps:[`V_oc,module = 37.1 × [1 − 0.0027 × (−5 − 25)] = 37.1 × 1.081 = 40.11 V`,`V_oc,string = 4 × 40.11 = 160.4 V`],answer:`160.4 V: too high for a 150 V controller. Three modules in series (120.3 V) would fit.`},{title:`Daily energy`,problem:`A 1.6 kW array (γ −0.35 %/°C) runs with cells at 60 °C; site PSH = 4.5; soiling 0.95, wiring 0.97, mismatch 0.98. Estimate its daily DC energy.`,steps:[`Temperature factor = 1 − 0.0035 × 35 = 0.8775`,`k = 0.8775 × 0.95 × 0.97 × 0.98 = 0.7925`,`E = 1600 × 4.5 × 0.7925 = 5706 Wh`],answer:`About 5.7 kWh per day before controller and battery losses.`}],applications:`String design (S2.M2.L1), array sizing (S2.M3.L2), controller selection (S2.M2.L2) and commissioning measurements (S2.M5.L1) all use these formulas. The laboratory's Design tab shows each of them with your numbers.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Using V_oc at 25 °C to check the controller’s voltage limit.`,correction:`Use the coldest expected temperature; voltage rises in the cold.`},{mistake:`Applying the temperature coefficient to the ambient instead of the cell temperature.`,correction:`Estimate the cell temperature first (NOCT method).`},{mistake:`Multiplying PSH by the nameplate power with no derating.`,correction:`Apply temperature, soiling, wiring and mismatch factors.`}],demo:{title:`Predict, then check`,text:`In the I–V laboratory’s measurement task, predict a string’s V_oc, I_sc and P_mp for the given conditions, then check them against the tracer.`,link:e.solar(`Solar laboratory: measurement task`,`iv`),embed:`array-config`},activities:[e.solar(`PV I–V laboratory: measurement task`,`iv`),e.build(`Build project G: solar inverter (MPPT stage)`,`bld-g`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`I_sc of a 13.8 A module at 700 W/m², 25 °C (A)?`,answer:9.66,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`13.8 × 0.7.`},{id:`k2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`V_oc of a 49.6 V module (β −0.27 %/°C) at 0 °C (V)?`,answer:52.95,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`49.6 × (1 + 0.0027 × 25) = 52.95 V.`},{id:`k3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Daily energy: 2 kW array, PSH 5, k = 0.8 (kWh)?`,answer:8,tol:.01,unit:`kWh`,explain:`2 × 5 × 0.8.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The highest string voltage occurs…`,options:[`on a cold, bright morning`,`at noon in summer`,`at night`,`when the battery is full`],answer:0,explain:`Cold cells and full light give the highest V_oc.`}],summary:[`I_sc ∝ G, with a small positive temperature coefficient.`,`V_oc and V_mp fall with temperature (β < 0): highest when cold.`,`P ≈ P_STC·(G/1000)·[1 + γ(T − 25)].`,`Strings: series multiplies voltage; parallel multiplies current.`,`E_day = P_STC × PSH × k, with k for temperature, soiling, wiring, mismatch.`],homework:{id:`S2.M1.L3.hw`,title:`PV calculations`,brief:`Illustrative 550 W module: V_oc 49.6 V, V_mp 41.7 V, I_sc 14.0 A, β −0.27 %/°C, γ −0.35 %/°C, NOCT 45 °C. Site: coldest 2 °C, hottest 38 °C, PSH 5.2.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Module V_oc on the coldest morning (V)?`,answer:52.68,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`49.6 × (1 + 0.0027 × 23) = 49.6 × 1.0621 = 52.68 V.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Maximum modules in series under a 250 V controller limit?`,answer:4,tol:.001,unit:``,explain:`250/52.68 = 4.75 → 4.`},{id:`h3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Hot cell temperature at 800 W/m² (°C)?`,answer:63,tol:.01,unit:`°C`,explain:`38 + 25 = 63 °C.`},{id:`h4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Module V_mp at that cell temperature (V)?`,answer:37.42,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`41.7 × (1 − 0.0027 × 38) = 41.7 × 0.8974 = 37.42 V.`}],written:[{prompt:`A 48 V battery charges at up to 57.6 V and the MPPT controller needs the PV voltage at least 5 V above the battery. Is a string of 2 of these modules enough on the hottest day? Show your reasoning.`,model:`Required: 57.6 + 5 = 62.6 V. Two modules at 63 °C give 2 × 37.42 = 74.8 V > 62.6 V, so yes; one module (37.4 V) would not be. Combined with h2, 2–4 modules in series are valid for this site and controller.`}]}}}],quiz:{pass:.7,questions:[{id:`q1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The light-generated current of a cell is proportional to…`,options:[`irradiance`,`the battery voltage`,`the cable size`,`the inverter rating`],answer:0,explain:`More photons, more current.`},{id:`q2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Fill factor: V_oc 40 V, I_sc 10 A, P_max 312 W?`,answer:.78,tol:.01,unit:``,explain:`312/400.`},{id:`q3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Cell temperature: NOCT 46 °C, ambient 28 °C, 800 W/m² (°C)?`,answer:54,tol:.01,unit:`°C`,explain:`28 + 26.`},{id:`q4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`V_oc of a 37.1 V module (β −0.27 %/°C) at −10 °C (V)?`,answer:40.61,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`37.1 × 1.0945 = 40.61 V.`},{id:`q5`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`P of a 400 W module (γ −0.35 %/°C) at 800 W/m² and 50 °C (W)?`,answer:292,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`400 × 0.8 × (1 − 0.0875) = 292 W.`},{id:`q6`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which datasheet limit constrains the number of modules in series?`,options:[`Maximum system voltage`,`Maximum series fuse`,`NOCT`,`Efficiency`],answer:0,explain:`Cold string V_oc must stay below it.`},{id:`q7`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Daily energy: 3 kW array, PSH 4, k = 0.78 (kWh)?`,answer:9.36,tol:.01,unit:`kWh`,explain:`3 × 4 × 0.78.`},{id:`q8`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Bypass diodes are found…`,options:[`in the module junction box`,`in the inverter output`,`in the battery`,`in the combiner fuse`],answer:0,explain:`Usually three per module.`}]},assignment:{id:`S2.M1.asg`,title:`Module assignment: characterising an array`,brief:`Six illustrative 400 W modules (V_oc 37.1 V, V_mp 31.0 V, I_sc 13.8 A, β −0.27 %/°C, α +0.048 %/°C, γ −0.35 %/°C, NOCT 45 °C) in two strings of three. Site: coldest −2 °C, hottest 36 °C, PSH 4.8; soiling 0.95, wiring 0.97, mismatch 0.98.`,tasks:[{id:`a1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`String V_oc on the coldest morning (V)?`,answer:119.4,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`3 × 37.1 × (1 + 0.0027 × 27) = 3 × 39.80 = 119.4 V.`},{id:`a2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Hot cell temperature at 800 W/m² (°C)?`,answer:61,tol:.01,unit:`°C`,explain:`36 + 25.`},{id:`a3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`String V_mp when hot (V)?`,answer:83.96,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`3 × 31.0 × (1 − 0.0027 × 36) = 3 × 27.99 = 83.96 V.`},{id:`a4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Array I_sc at STC (A)?`,answer:27.6,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`2 × 13.8.`},{id:`a5`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Daily DC energy (kWh)?`,answer:9.093,tol:.02,unit:`kWh`,explain:`k = (1 − 0.0035 × 36) × 0.95 × 0.97 × 0.98 = 0.874 × 0.9031 = 0.7893; E = 2400 × 4.8 × 0.7893 = 9093 Wh.`}],written:[{prompt:`Which controller voltage class (100, 150 or 250 V) would you choose, and why?`,model:`The cold string V_oc is 119.4 V, above 100 V, so a 150 V controller is the smallest suitable class, leaving about 25 % headroom; 250 V would also work but is unnecessary unless the strings may be lengthened.`}]},practicals:[{id:`S2.M1.P1`,title:`PV I–V laboratory: predict and verify a string`,description:`Complete the I–V laboratory’s measurement task: predict a string’s V_oc, I_sc and P_mp for given conditions within tolerance.`,link:e.solar(`PV I–V laboratory`,`iv`),evidence:{kind:`activity`,id:`solar:iv`}}]},{id:`S2.M2`,title:`Arrays, controllers and conversion`,summary:`How modules are combined into strings and arrays, what shading and mismatch do, how MPPT and PWM charge controllers work, and how off-grid, grid-tied and hybrid inverters are built.`,lessons:[{id:`S2.M2.L1`,title:`Series and parallel panel configurations`,minutes:40,content:{objectives:[`Configure modules in series strings and parallel strings for a required voltage and current.`,`Determine the allowed string length from the cold V_oc and hot V_mp limits.`,`Explain mismatch, partial shading and the action of bypass diodes.`,`Decide when string fuses are needed and size them.`],introduction:`A single module rarely matches what a controller or inverter needs. Modules are therefore connected in series into [[pv-string|strings]] to raise the voltage, and strings in parallel to raise the current. The configuration must keep the voltage inside a window set by temperature and the equipment, and it must protect every string from faults fed by the others.`,lecture:`### Series strings

Modules in series carry the same current; their voltages add:

- V_string = n × V_module;
- I_string = I_module.

### Parallel strings

Strings in parallel share the same voltage; their currents add:

- I_array = N_strings × I_string.

Parallel strings must be **identical**: the same number and type of modules, the same orientation. A string with one module fewer has a lower voltage and drags the others away from their maximum power point.

### The voltage window

The string length n must satisfy two limits:

1. **Upper limit (cold):** n × V_oc,cold ≤ the maximum input voltage of the controller or inverter, and ≤ the module's maximum system voltage. Exceeding it on a cold morning can destroy the equipment instantly.
2. **Lower limit (hot):** n × V_mp,hot ≥ the minimum working voltage. For an MPPT battery charger this is the battery's charging voltage plus a headroom (often about 5 V; see the controller's data). For a grid inverter it is the bottom of its MPPT voltage range.

Any n between the two limits is valid. The laboratory chooses the n that uses the fewest modules for the required power.

### Mismatch and shading

In a string the same current must flow through every module. If one module produces less current (dirt, shade, a fault):

- without protection, the weak module would be driven into reverse voltage and could overheat (a hot spot);
- **[[bypass-diode|bypass diodes]]** across each third of the module start conducting, so the string current passes around the shaded cells;
- the string loses that section's voltage, and its P–V curve gains a second, local peak. A simple MPPT can settle on the wrong peak (lesson L2).

Avoid mixing shaded and unshaded modules in one string; use separate strings (or MPPT inputs) for different orientations.

### Reverse current and string fuses

If one string develops a short circuit, all the other parallel strings drive current backwards into it: up to (N − 1) × I_sc. With **two** strings this is one string's current, which modules are designed to withstand. With three or more strings it can exceed the module's **maximum series fuse rating**. Then each string needs a [[string-fuse|string fuse]], typically rated:

**1.56 × I_sc ≤ I_n ≤ maximum series fuse rating**

(1.56 = 1.25 × 1.25, for high irradiance and continuous operation; standards differ slightly — some use 1.5 × I_sc — so follow the applicable one.)

### Combiner and isolation

Parallel strings meet in a [[combiner|combiner box]] containing the string fuses (on both poles where required), often surge protection, and a lockable **DC isolator** rated for the cold V_oc and 1.25 × the array's I_sc.`,technical:`**Why 1.25 twice?** One factor of 1.25 allows for irradiance above 1000 W/m² (cloud-edge effects and reflection can briefly raise it); the second for the conductor and fuse running continuously near rating. Cable and fuse ratings for PV circuits therefore start from 1.25 × I_sc and 1.56 × I_sc respectively.

**Multiple MPPT inputs.** Many inverters have two or more independent MPPT inputs. Strings with different orientations or lengths go on separate inputs so that each is held at its own MPP.

**Module-level electronics.** Optimisers and microinverters give each module its own MPPT, which reduces shading losses and allows module-level shutdown, at higher cost and with more components on the roof.`,diagrams:[{id:`string-config`,caption:`Two strings of three modules with bypass diodes and string fuses, joined in a combiner with a DC isolator.`}],formulas:[{name:`String length window`,expr:`⌈V_min / V_mp,hot⌉ ≤ n ≤ ⌊V_max / V_oc,cold⌋`,where:[[`V_min`,`minimum working voltage of the controller/inverter`,`V`],[`V_max`,`lower of equipment and module maximum voltage`,`V`]]},{name:`Reverse current into a faulty string`,expr:`I_rev = (N − 1) × 1.25 × I_sc`,where:[[`N`,`number of parallel strings`,`—`]]},{name:`String fuse window`,expr:`1.56 × I_sc ≤ I_n ≤ I_max,series fuse`,where:[]}],examples:[{title:`String window`,problem:`Illustrative 400 W modules: V_oc,cold = 38.40 V, V_mp,hot = 28.07 V. MPPT controller limit 150 V; battery charging at 26.6 V with 5 V headroom. Find the allowed string lengths.`,steps:[`n_max = ⌊150 / 38.40⌋ = ⌊3.91⌋ = 3`,`n_min = ⌈(26.6 + 5) / 28.07⌉ = ⌈1.13⌉ = 2`,`Allowed: 2 or 3 in series`],answer:`Two or three modules per string.`},{title:`Are string fuses needed?`,problem:`Three parallel strings of modules with I_sc = 13.8 A and a maximum series fuse rating of 25 A. Are fuses needed, and what rating (sizes 20, 25, 32 A)?`,steps:[`I_rev = (3 − 1) × 1.25 × 13.8 = 34.5 A > 25 A → fuses needed`,`I_n ≥ 1.56 × 13.8 = 21.5 A and ≤ 25 A → 25 A`],answer:`Yes: a 25 A DC-rated fuse in each string.`}],applications:`Every PV array design. The laboratory's Design tab computes the window, chooses the configuration and decides on string fuses; its troubleshooting tab includes a shaded module, an open string and a reversed string.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Checking string voltage at 25 °C only.`,correction:`Check cold V_oc against the maximum and hot V_mp against the minimum.`},{mistake:`Paralleling strings of different lengths or orientations on one MPPT input.`,correction:`Keep parallel strings identical; use separate MPPT inputs otherwise.`},{mistake:`Omitting string fuses with three or more parallel strings.`,correction:`Check reverse current against the module’s maximum series fuse rating.`}],demo:{title:`Array configuration`,text:`Change modules in series, parallel strings and the coldest temperature; watch the cold string V_oc against the controller limit and the array current.`,link:e.solar(`Solar laboratory: system design`,`design`),embed:`array-config`},activities:[e.solar(`PV I–V laboratory: shade one module`,`iv`),e.solar(`Troubleshooting: shading and open strings`,`faults`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Max modules in series: limit 250 V, V_oc,cold 52.7 V?`,answer:4,tol:.001,unit:``,explain:`250/52.7 = 4.74 → 4.`},{id:`k2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Reverse current into a faulty string with 4 strings of I_sc 9.3 A (A)?`,answer:34.88,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`3 × 1.25 × 9.3 = 34.9 A.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A shaded module’s bypass diode conducts. The string…`,options:[`loses about that section’s voltage and its P–V curve gains a second peak`,`stops completely`,`doubles its current`,`is unaffected`],answer:0,explain:`Current bypasses the shaded cells.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`With two parallel strings, string fuses are usually…`,options:[`not required, because one string’s reverse current is within the module rating`,`always required`,`replaced by RCDs`,`only on the negative`],answer:0,explain:`(2 − 1) × 1.25 × I_sc is normally below the maximum series fuse.`}],summary:[`Series adds voltage; parallel strings add current; parallel strings must match.`,`String length window: cold V_oc below the maximum, hot V_mp above the minimum.`,`Shading makes bypass diodes conduct and creates multiple power peaks.`,`Three or more strings may need string fuses: 1.56·I_sc ≤ I_n ≤ max series fuse.`,`Combiner: fuses, surge protection, lockable DC isolator.`],homework:{id:`S2.M2.L1.hw`,title:`String design`,brief:`Illustrative 550 W modules: V_oc 49.6 V, V_mp 41.7 V, I_sc 14.0 A, β −0.27 %/°C, max series fuse 25 A. Coldest 2 °C; hot cell 63 °C. Grid inverter: 600 V maximum, MPPT range from 120 V.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Maximum modules in series?`,answer:11,tol:.001,unit:``,explain:`V_oc,cold = 52.68 V; 600/52.68 = 11.4 → 11.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Minimum modules in series?`,answer:4,tol:.001,unit:``,explain:`V_mp,hot = 37.42 V; 120/37.42 = 3.2 → 4.`},{id:`h3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Three parallel strings: are string fuses needed?`,options:[`Yes: 2 × 1.25 × 14 = 35 A > 25 A`,`No`],answer:0,explain:`Reverse current exceeds the module rating.`}],written:[{prompt:`Why should modules facing east and west not be in the same string?`,model:`They receive different irradiance at different times, so their currents differ; in one string the weaker modules limit the current or are bypassed, losing energy. East and west strings should be on separate MPPT inputs (or the same MPPT only as separate, identical parallel strings with care).`}]}}},{id:`S2.M2.L2`,title:`MPPT and PWM charge controllers`,minutes:40,content:{objectives:[`Explain how a PWM charge controller operates and why it fixes the array at battery voltage.`,`Explain MPPT as a DC-DC converter and describe the perturb-and-observe algorithm.`,`Compare the energy harvested by PWM and MPPT for a given array and battery.`,`Describe the bulk, absorption and float charging stages and where their set-points come from.`],introduction:`The [[charge-controller|charge controller]] sits between the PV array and the battery. It must harvest as much energy as possible and charge the battery correctly without overcharging it. Two types dominate: simple **PWM** controllers and **MPPT** controllers. The difference between them is visible directly on the P–V curve.`,lecture:`### PWM controllers

A PWM controller is essentially a switch between the array and the battery:

- While charging, the switch is on and the array is connected **directly to the battery**. The array therefore operates at **battery voltage** (plus small drops), wherever that falls on its curve.
- Near full charge, the switch is pulsed (pulse-width modulated) to hold the battery at its absorption or float voltage.

This works well when the module's V_mp is a little above battery voltage: one 36-cell "12 V" module (V_mp ≈ 18 V) per 12 V of battery. With larger modules (60/72-cell or half-cell, V_mp 30–42 V) most of the power is wasted.

### MPPT controllers

An MPPT controller is a **DC-DC buck converter**:

- It holds the array at its maximum power point voltage V_mp, which can be much higher than the battery.
- It converts that power down to battery voltage at a higher current: P_out = η × P_mp, so I_battery = η × P_mp / V_battery.
- It allows long, high-voltage strings, reducing PV cable current and losses.

Typical efficiencies are 95–98 %.

### Perturb and observe

The [[perturb-observe|perturb-and-observe]] algorithm:

1. measure power at the present operating voltage;
2. change the voltage a little (perturb);
3. if power rose, keep going the same way; if it fell, reverse.

It climbs the P–V curve and oscillates around the peak. On a shaded, multi-peak curve it can stop on a **local** peak; better controllers periodically **sweep** the whole curve to find the global maximum.

### Charging stages

Lead-acid and many lithium batteries are charged in stages:

- **Bulk:** maximum available current until the voltage reaches the absorption set-point.
- **Absorption:** voltage held constant while the current tapers.
- **Float:** a lower voltage that keeps a full lead-acid battery topped up. LiFePO₄ batteries often need no float, or a low one.

The set-points — absorption and float voltages, current limits, temperature compensation — come from the **battery manufacturer's datasheet**. With lithium batteries, the battery management system may also command the charger.

### Sizing a controller

- **Voltage:** maximum PV input ≥ the array's cold V_oc (the most important check).
- **Current:** MPPT output rating ≥ P_array × η / V_battery; PWM rating ≥ 1.25 × I_sc,array.`,technical:`**Why buck, not boost?** Most MPPT battery chargers are buck converters, so the array voltage must stay above the battery voltage by a margin under all conditions: hence the hot V_mp minimum in lesson L1. Grid inverters typically use a boost stage instead, so their MPPT range can start lower.

**PWM harvest example.** The laboratory's model of the illustrative 400 W module at 1000 W/m² and 45 °C gives about 370 W at its MPP but only about 175 W at 13.2 V: PWM would harvest less than half. For a matched 36-cell 100 W module the PWM harvest is about 75–85 % of the MPP power.

**Controller self-protection.** Exceeding the maximum PV voltage is usually not survivable: the input semiconductors break down before any protection can act. That is why cold V_oc is a hard design limit.`,diagrams:[{id:`mppt-pwm`,caption:`On the P–V curve: MPPT holds the array at its peak; PWM fixes it at battery voltage.`},{id:`charge-stages`,caption:`Bulk (constant current), absorption (constant voltage) and float stages.`}],formulas:[{name:`MPPT output current`,expr:`I_bat = η · P_mp / V_bat`,where:[[`η`,`controller efficiency`,`—`]]},{name:`PWM operating power`,expr:`P_PWM = V_bat · I(V_bat + V_drop)`,where:[[`I(V)`,`array current at that voltage, from its I–V curve`,`A`]]},{name:`Controller current rating`,expr:`MPPT: I ≥ P_array · η / V_sys;  PWM: I ≥ 1.25 · I_sc,array`,where:[]}],examples:[{title:`MPPT output current`,problem:`A 1.6 kW array at its MPP charges a 24 V battery at 26.4 V through an MPPT controller with η = 0.97. Find the charging current.`,steps:[`P_out = 0.97 × 1600 = 1552 W`,`I = 1552 / 26.4 = 58.8 A`],answer:`58.8 A: a 60 A or 70 A controller.`},{title:`PWM versus MPPT`,problem:`A module delivers 370 W at its MPP but 13.2 A at 13.2 V. Compare the power to a 12 V battery with PWM and with MPPT (η = 0.97).`,steps:[`PWM: P = 13.2 × 13.2 = 174 W`,`MPPT: P = 0.97 × 370 = 359 W`,`MPPT harvests 359 / 174 = 2.06 times as much`],answer:`About twice the energy with MPPT for this module.`}],applications:`Off-grid and hybrid systems (S2.M4), the MPPT stage of build project G, and the PWM-mismatch troubleshooting scenario all use these principles. Course P5 develops battery charging in depth.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Using large 60/72-cell modules with a PWM controller.`,correction:`PWM fixes the array at battery voltage; use MPPT, or modules matched to the battery.`},{mistake:`Sizing an MPPT controller by array I_sc.`,correction:`Its output current is P × η / V_battery; its input voltage limit is checked against cold V_oc.`},{mistake:`Using generic charging voltages.`,correction:`Take set-points from the battery datasheet (and the BMS for lithium).`}],demo:{title:`MPPT tracking and PWM`,text:`Shade one module and watch the perturb-and-observe tracker settle on a local peak; set a battery voltage to see where a PWM controller would operate.`,link:e.solar(`Solar laboratory: PV I–V tracer with PWM`,`iv`),embed:`mppt-track`},activities:[e.build(`Build project G: solar inverter with MPPT`,`bld-g`),e.solar(`Troubleshooting: PWM mismatch`,`faults`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`MPPT current: 2.2 kW array, η 0.97, battery 28.8 V (A)?`,answer:74.1,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`2134/28.8 = 74.1 A.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`With a PWM controller the array operates at…`,options:[`battery voltage`,`its MPP voltage`,`its V_oc`,`zero volts`],answer:0,explain:`It is switched directly onto the battery.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Perturb and observe reverses direction when…`,options:[`power falls after a step`,`power rises`,`the battery is full`,`current is zero`],answer:0,explain:`It climbs the power hill.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Absorption and float voltages come from…`,options:[`the battery manufacturer’s datasheet`,`the module datasheet`,`a fixed universal value`,`the inverter’s nameplate`],answer:0,explain:`They depend on the battery chemistry and product.`}],summary:[`PWM: array at battery voltage; suits matched 36-cell-per-12 V modules.`,`MPPT: DC-DC converter at the array’s MPP; I_bat = η·P/V_bat.`,`P&O climbs the P–V curve; sweeps find the global peak under shading.`,`Bulk, absorption, float: set-points from the battery datasheet.`,`Check the controller’s PV voltage limit against cold V_oc first.`],homework:{id:`S2.M2.L2.hw`,title:`Controller selection`,brief:`Array: 2 strings × 3 illustrative 400 W modules (cold V_oc 119.4 V), 48 V battery charging at 54 V; controller classes 100/150/250 V and 30/40/50/60 A.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`MPPT output current at full array power, η 0.97 (A)?`,answer:43.1,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`2400 × 0.97 / 54 = 43.1 A.`},{id:`h2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Controller choice?`,options:[`150 V, 50 A`,`100 V, 40 A`,`250 V, 30 A`,`150 V, 30 A`],answer:0,explain:`119.4 V > 100 V → 150 V; 43.1 A → 50 A.`}],written:[{prompt:`Explain why an MPPT controller allows thinner PV cables than PWM for the same power.`,model:`MPPT can accept a much higher string voltage, so the same power flows at lower current; cable losses (I²R) and voltage drop fall with the square and first power of current respectively, so a smaller cross-section meets the drop limit.`}]}}},{id:`S2.M2.L3`,title:`Solar inverter architecture`,minutes:35,content:{objectives:[`Describe the power stages of off-grid, grid-tied and hybrid solar inverters.`,`Explain anti-islanding and why grid-tied inverters cannot provide back-up on their own.`,`Compare string inverters, microinverters and optimisers.`,`Relate inverter ratings (DC input, MPPT range, AC output, surge) to system design.`],introduction:`Course S1 built inverters from the bridge up. This lesson places them in solar systems. An off-grid inverter makes its own AC from a battery; a grid-tied inverter follows the grid and pushes PV power into it; a hybrid inverter does both and manages a battery. Their architectures decide what each system can and cannot do during an outage.`,lecture:`### Off-grid (battery) inverter

**Battery → (DC bus) → bridge → filter (→ transformer) → AC loads**

- It forms the AC voltage and frequency itself ("grid-forming").
- It must supply every load's surge (motors, compressors).
- It has low-voltage disconnect ([[lvd|LVD]]) to protect the battery.
- The PV charge controller is often separate (or built in as an MPPT stage), with a charger from a generator input.

### Grid-tied inverter

**PV array → MPPT DC-DC (usually boost) → DC link → bridge → filter → grid**

- It synchronises to the grid and injects current in phase with it ("grid-following").
- It has no battery, so it can only deliver what the array produces at that moment.
- **[[anti-islanding|Anti-islanding]]:** it must detect loss of the grid and stop within a short time, so that it cannot energise lines that workers believe are dead. During an outage it produces nothing, even in full sun.
- Ratings to check: maximum DC voltage, MPPT voltage range, maximum input current per MPPT, AC output power and the grid-connection rules of the network operator.

### Hybrid inverter

**PV → MPPT → DC bus ← battery (bidirectional converter) → bridge ⇄ grid and backed-up loads**

- With the grid present it uses PV for the loads, charges the battery and exports any surplus where allowed ([[self-consumption]]).
- When the grid fails it disconnects from the grid (anti-islanding) with an internal or external **[[transfer-switch|transfer switch]]** and supplies the backed-up loads from the battery and PV.
- Many accept a generator input as a second AC source.

### String inverters, microinverters and optimisers

| Type | MPPT | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| String inverter | One per input (often two or more inputs) | Lowest cost per watt; shading of one module affects its string |
| Microinverter | One per module, AC from the roof | Module-level MPPT and shutdown; more units on the roof |
| Optimiser + inverter | DC-DC per module | Module-level MPPT, string inverter for conversion |

### Ratings in design

- **Off-grid:** continuous power ≥ 1.25 × peak load; surge capability ≥ starting loads; DC input voltage = battery system voltage.
- **Grid-tied:** DC/AC ratio typically 1.1–1.3 (arrays rarely reach nameplate power); string window within the MPPT range.
- **Hybrid:** both sets of checks, plus battery charge and discharge power and the backed-up load list.`,technical:`**Transformerless grid inverters.** Many grid inverters have no galvanic isolation between PV and grid. This affects earthing arrangements and requires residual-current monitoring inside the inverter, and it is why some module types (and frame earthing rules) are specified with them. These are installation-standard topics (course S3).

**Reactive power and grid codes.** Network operators increasingly require inverters to support the grid: limits on export, power-factor control, ride-through of short disturbances. Settings are made at commissioning to the operator's requirements.

**Build projects.** Build project G is a solar inverter (MPPT stage + battery + sine inverter); build project H is a hybrid with grid/generator selection and a transfer relay. They implement simplified versions of these architectures in the simulator.`,diagrams:[{id:`system-types`,caption:`Off-grid, grid-tied and hybrid architectures.`},{id:`hybrid-paths`,caption:`Energy paths of the hybrid inverter built in project H: grid or generator, transfer relay, charger, battery and inverter.`}],formulas:[{name:`Off-grid inverter rating`,expr:`P_inv ≥ 1.25 · P_peak;  surge ≥ P_peak + largest starting increment`,where:[]},{name:`Grid inverter sizing`,expr:`P_AC ≈ P_array / (DC/AC ratio, typically 1.1–1.3)`,where:[]}],examples:[{title:`Grid inverter size`,problem:`A 6.6 kWp array is connected to a grid inverter with a DC/AC ratio of 1.2. What AC rating is indicated?`,steps:[`P_AC = 6600 / 1.2 = 5500 W`,`Choose the nearest standard size at or above it, for example 6 kW (or 5 kW if a ratio of 1.32 is acceptable to the manufacturer)`],answer:`About 5.5 kW AC, so a 6 kW inverter (check the inverter’s maximum DC power and input limits).`},{title:`Off-grid inverter`,problem:`Peak simultaneous load 1310 W; a 1 kW steriliser and a fridge (starting 600 W, running 120 W). Inverter surge factor 2. Choose from 1500, 2000, 3000 W.`,steps:[`Continuous: 1.25 × 1310 = 1638 W`,`Surge: 1310 + (600 − 120) = 1790 W → 1790/2 = 895 W (not limiting)`,`Next size ≥ 1638 W → 2000 W`],answer:`2000 W.`}],applications:`Choosing the inverter is part of every S2.M3 design. The laboratory's energy-management tab shows anti-islanding during a grid outage, and how a hybrid system rides through it.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Expecting a grid-tied PV system to power the house during a blackout.`,correction:`Anti-islanding stops it; back-up needs a battery and a hybrid or off-grid inverter.`},{mistake:`Sizing an off-grid inverter by average power.`,correction:`Size by peak simultaneous power and starting surges.`},{mistake:`Ignoring the grid operator’s connection rules.`,correction:`Grid connection needs approval and the specified settings.`}],demo:{title:`Energy flows by system type`,text:`Choose the off-grid, hybrid and grid-tied examples and watch how PV, battery, grid and load interact over a day.`,link:e.solar(`Solar laboratory: energy management`,`energy`),embed:`energy-day`},activities:[e.build(`Build project G: solar inverter`,`bld-g`),e.build(`Build project H: hybrid inverter`,`bld-h`),e.solar(`Troubleshooting: no power during an outage`,`faults`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A grid-forming inverter…`,options:[`creates its own voltage and frequency (off-grid)`,`only follows the grid`,`has no bridge`,`cannot supply loads`],answer:0,explain:`Off-grid and hybrid inverters in back-up mode form the grid.`},{id:`k2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`AC rating for an 8 kWp array at DC/AC ratio 1.25 (kW)?`,answer:6.4,tol:.01,unit:`kW`,explain:`8/1.25.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Why must grid-tied inverters stop during an outage?`,options:[`To avoid energising lines workers believe are dead`,`To save the battery`,`Because PV stops`,`To reduce noise`],answer:0,explain:`Anti-islanding protects line workers.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Module-level MPPT is provided by…`,options:[`microinverters or optimisers`,`string fuses`,`the battery`,`the transfer switch`],answer:0,explain:`Each module has its own tracker.`}],summary:[`Off-grid: battery → bridge → AC; grid-forming, surge and LVD matter.`,`Grid-tied: PV → MPPT → bridge → grid; anti-islanding, no back-up.`,`Hybrid: PV, battery and grid on one DC bus; back-up via a transfer switch.`,`String inverter vs microinverter vs optimiser: where MPPT happens.`,`Ratings: continuous, surge, DC voltage, MPPT range, DC/AC ratio.`],homework:{id:`S2.M2.L3.hw`,title:`Choosing an architecture`,brief:`A clinic is connected to an unreliable grid with daily outages of several hours and wants to use solar energy.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which architecture supplies the clinic during outages?`,options:[`Hybrid with battery`,`Grid-tied without battery`,`PWM controller only`,`Microinverters without battery`],answer:0,explain:`Back-up needs storage and grid-forming capability.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Backed-up loads total 1.2 kW peak. Minimum continuous inverter rating with 1.25 margin (W)?`,answer:1500,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`1.25 × 1200.`}],written:[{prompt:`Explain what happens in the hybrid system at the moment the grid fails, and why.`,model:`The inverter detects the loss (voltage/frequency), opens its grid relay or the transfer switch so it is isolated from the network (anti-islanding), then forms its own AC from the battery and PV to supply the backed-up loads; when the grid returns and is stable it resynchronises and reconnects.`}]}}}],quiz:{pass:.7,questions:[{id:`q1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`String V_oc of 9 modules at 52.68 V cold (V)?`,answer:474.1,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`9 × 52.68.`},{id:`q2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Minimum string length: MPPT minimum 150 V, V_mp,hot 28.07 V?`,answer:6,tol:.001,unit:``,explain:`150/28.07 = 5.34 → 6.`},{id:`q3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`String fuse minimum for I_sc 9.3 A (A)?`,answer:14.51,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`1.56 × 9.3.`},{id:`q4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A string has one heavily shaded module. A simple P&O controller may…`,options:[`settle on a local power peak`,`trip the RCD`,`charge the battery faster`,`reverse the current`],answer:0,explain:`Multi-peak curves can trap P&O.`},{id:`q5`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`MPPT output current: 1.2 kW array, η 0.96, 14 V battery (A)?`,answer:82.29,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`1152/14.`},{id:`q6`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which pairing suits a PWM controller on 12 V?`,options:[`A 36-cell module`,`A 72-cell module`,`A 144-half-cell module`,`Any module`],answer:0,explain:`V_mp ≈ 18 V suits a 12 V battery.`},{id:`q7`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`During a grid outage a grid-tied inverter without battery…`,options:[`stops producing`,`supplies the house`,`charges a battery`,`exports at full power`],answer:0,explain:`Anti-islanding.`},{id:`q8`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The stage in which the voltage is held constant while current tapers is…`,options:[`absorption`,`bulk`,`float`,`equalisation only`],answer:0,explain:`Absorption.`}]},assignment:{id:`S2.M2.asg`,title:`Module assignment: configuring an array and controller`,brief:`Required array ≈ 2.1 kW from illustrative 550 W modules (V_oc,cold 51.34 V at 12 °C coldest, V_mp,hot 37.76 V, I_sc 14.0 A, max series fuse 25 A). 24 V battery charging at 28.8 V. Controller classes: 100/150/250 V; 50/60/70/80/100 A; η 0.97.`,tasks:[{id:`a1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Modules needed?`,answer:4,tol:.001,unit:``,explain:`2100/550 = 3.8 → 4.`},{id:`a2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Maximum in series with a 150 V controller?`,answer:2,tol:.001,unit:``,explain:`150/51.34 = 2.92 → 2.`},{id:`a3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Minimum in series (28.8 + 5 V)?`,answer:1,tol:.001,unit:``,explain:`33.8/37.76 = 0.9 → 1.`},{id:`a4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`With 2 × 2, MPPT output current at 24 V nominal (A)?`,answer:88.92,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`2200 × 0.97 / 24 = 88.9 A → 100 A controller.`},{id:`a5`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`String fuses for 2 parallel strings?`,options:[`Not required: 1.25 × 14 = 17.5 A ≤ 25 A`,`Required`],answer:0,explain:`One string’s reverse current is within the rating.`}],written:[{prompt:`Would a 250 V controller allow a better configuration? Discuss.`,model:`With 250 V, 4 in series (cold V_oc 205 V) is possible as a single string: lower array current (14 A instead of 28 A), thinner PV cable and no combiner, at the cost of a higher, more hazardous DC voltage and a controller that accepts it. Output current is the same (≈ 89 A at 24 V) because it depends on power.`}]},practicals:[{id:`S2.M2.P1`,title:`Build project G: solar inverter with MPPT stage`,description:`Design, assemble and test the solar inverter in the build laboratory, including the MPPT stage and battery charging.`,link:e.build(`Build project G`,`bld-g`),evidence:{kind:`project`,id:`bld-g`}},{id:`S2.M2.P2`,title:`Array and controller configuration assessment`,description:`Configure strings and select a controller for a given site and battery. All answers must be correct.`,task:{id:`s2-strings`,kind:`calc`,title:`Strings, fuses and controller`,brief:`Illustrative 400 W modules: V_oc 37.1 V, V_mp 31.0 V, I_sc 13.8 A, β −0.27 %/°C, NOCT 45 °C, max series fuse 25 A. Site: coldest −8 °C, hottest 40 °C. 48 V battery charging at 54 V; MPPT needs 5 V headroom. Controller classes 150/250 V. Six modules required.`,questions:[{id:`c1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Module V_oc on the coldest morning (V)?`,answer:40.41,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`37.1 × (1 + 0.0027 × 33) = 37.1 × 1.0891 = 40.41 V.`},{id:`c2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Hot cell temperature at 800 W/m² (°C)?`,answer:65,tol:.01,unit:`°C`,explain:`40 + 25.`},{id:`c3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Module V_mp when hot (V)?`,answer:27.65,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`31.0 × (1 − 0.0027 × 40) = 31.0 × 0.892 = 27.65 V.`},{id:`c4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Minimum modules in series?`,answer:3,tol:.001,unit:``,explain:`(54 + 5)/27.65 = 2.13 → 3.`},{id:`c5`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Maximum in series with a 150 V controller?`,answer:3,tol:.001,unit:``,explain:`150/40.41 = 3.71 → 3.`},{id:`c6`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Configuration and controller class?`,options:[`3 in series × 2 strings on a 150 V controller`,`6 in series on a 150 V controller`,`2 in series × 3 strings on 150 V`,`1 × 6 on 150 V`],answer:0,explain:`3 × 2 fits the 150 V window (cold V_oc 121 V); 6 in series (242 V) would need 250 V; 2 in series is below the minimum.`}],explain:`The string window comes from cold V_oc (maximum) and hot V_mp (minimum); then choose the configuration and check the controller voltage class.`},evidence:{kind:`task`,id:`s2-strings`}}]},{id:`S2.M3`,title:`System design from a load profile`,summary:`The complete sizing method: from a real list of appliances to daily energy and peak demand, then the inverter, the PV array and the battery bank, each with its formula, working and assumptions.`,lessons:[{id:`S2.M3.L1`,title:`Load profiles and energy budgets`,minutes:40,content:{objectives:[`Build a load profile from appliance power, quantity, duty and hours of use.`,`Calculate daily AC and DC energy and the energy drawn from the battery.`,`Determine peak simultaneous demand and starting surge.`,`Size the inverter and choose the system voltage.`],introduction:`Every solar design starts with the loads. Overestimate them and the system is needlessly expensive; underestimate them and it fails on the first cloudy evening. A [[load-profile|load profile]] lists each appliance with its power and when it runs. From it come two different numbers: the daily **energy** (which sizes the battery and the array) and the **peak power** (which sizes the inverter). In this module you will size a complete system by hand and check every number in the solar laboratory.`,lecture:`### Collecting the data

For each load record:

- **power** (W): from the nameplate or, better, measured with a power meter;
- **quantity**;
- **hours of use** and *when* (morning, daytime, evening, all day);
- **duty**: the fraction of its on-time it actually draws power. A refrigerator's compressor cycles, so it may run 30–50 % of the time;
- **starting power**: motors and compressors draw several times their running power for a moment when starting.

Reduce loads first: efficient appliances (LED lighting, inverter fridges) are always cheaper than extra modules and batteries.

### Daily energy

For each load: **E = P × qty × duty × hours**. Sum the AC loads and the DC loads separately.

Energy drawn from the battery:

**E_d = E_AC / η_inverter + E_DC**

The inverter's efficiency (and its idle consumption) adds to every AC watt-hour.

### Peak and surge

- **Peak simultaneous load:** the largest total running power of loads that can be on at the same time. The profile's timing matters: a pump at 06:00 and a TV at 20:00 do not add.
- **Surge:** the peak plus the largest single starting increment (P_start − P_run).

### The inverter

- **Continuous rating** ≥ 1.25 × peak (margin for heat and ageing).
- **Surge capability** (from the datasheet, often given as a multiple of rating for a few seconds) ≥ surge.

### System voltage

Higher battery voltage means lower current for the same power (F2.M2.L3). A common rule of thumb:

| Inverter | Battery voltage |
|---|---|
| up to about 1.2 kW | 12 V |
| up to about 3 kW | 24 V |
| larger | 48 V |

At 12 V a 1.5 kW inverter draws about 140 A; at 48 V about 36 A.`,technical:`**Profiles, not just totals.** The hour-by-hour profile matters for energy management (S2.M4): energy used at midday can come straight from PV, while evening energy must pass through the battery, with its losses. The laboratory draws the hourly profile from your windows of use.

**Measured data.** For existing buildings, a week of logged consumption is more reliable than nameplate estimates. Nameplate power is often a maximum; duty and standby must be estimated or measured.

**Values in this course.** The appliance library in the laboratory uses typical values for learning. A real design uses nameplate or measured values for the actual appliances.`,diagrams:[{id:`design-chain`,caption:`The sizing sequence: each step uses the result of the one before.`}],formulas:[{name:`Load energy`,expr:`E = P × qty × duty × hours`,where:[[`duty`,`fraction of on-time drawing power`,`—`]]},{name:`Energy at the battery`,expr:`E_d = E_AC / η_inv + E_DC`,where:[]},{name:`Inverter rating`,expr:`P_inv ≥ 1.25 · P_peak;  surge ≥ P_peak + max(P_start − P_run)`,where:[]}],examples:[{title:`A rural home`,problem:`Loads: 8 LED lamps 8 W 18–23 h; fridge 120 W, duty 0.4, all day (starting 600 W); TV 80 W 19–22 h; 4 phone chargers 10 W 18–22 h; laptop 60 W 9–13 h; 2 fans 50 W 12–17 h; pump 750 W 06–07 h (starting 2250 W); router 10 W all day. Inverter η = 0.9. Find the daily energy at the battery.`,steps:[`LEDs 8 × 8 × 5 = 320; fridge 120 × 0.4 × 24 = 1152; TV 80 × 3 = 240; chargers 4 × 10 × 4 = 160`,`Laptop 60 × 4 = 240; fans 2 × 50 × 5 = 500; pump 750 × 1 = 750; router 10 × 24 = 240`,`E_AC = 3602 Wh; E_d = 3602 / 0.9 = 4002 Wh`],answer:`3.60 kWh/day of AC energy; 4.00 kWh/day from the battery.`},{title:`Peak, surge and inverter`,problem:`For the same home, find the peak simultaneous load, the surge and the inverter rating (sizes 1000, 1500, 2000 W; surge factor 2).`,steps:[`06:00–07:00: pump 750 + fridge 120 + router 10 = 880 W (evening: 64 + 120 + 80 + 40 + 10 = 314 W)`,`Surge: 880 + (2250 − 750) = 2380 W`,`Continuous 1.25 × 880 = 1100 W; surge 2380 / 2 = 1190 W → 1500 W`],answer:`Peak 880 W, surge 2380 W: a 1500 W inverter; the pump’s start decides it. At 1.5 kW a 24 V system is chosen.`}],applications:`The solar laboratory’s Design tab builds exactly this profile; the example "Off-grid rural home" is the home in these examples. Changing a load updates every later number in the design.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Adding the running power of every appliance to get the peak.`,correction:`Only loads that can run at the same time add; use the time profile.`},{mistake:`Ignoring duty cycles (using a fridge’s full power for 24 h).`,correction:`Multiply by the fraction of time it runs, or measure its daily energy.`},{mistake:`Forgetting motor starting surges.`,correction:`Check the inverter’s surge capability against the largest starting load.`}],demo:{title:`Load profile`,text:`Choose an example system and read its hourly profile, daily energy, peak and surge; then edit the loads on the laboratory’s Design tab.`,link:e.solar(`Solar laboratory: load profile`,`design`),embed:`load-profile`},activities:[e.solar(`Solar laboratory: build your own load profile`,`design`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Daily energy of a 150 W freezer, duty 0.45, all day (Wh)?`,answer:1620,tol:.01,unit:`Wh`,explain:`150 × 0.45 × 24.`},{id:`k2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Energy at the battery for 2.4 kWh AC at η 0.92 (kWh)?`,answer:2.609,tol:.01,unit:`kWh`,explain:`2.4/0.92.`},{id:`k3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Inverter minimum continuous rating for a 1.8 kW peak (W)?`,answer:2250,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`1.25 × 1800.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which number sizes the inverter?`,options:[`Peak simultaneous power and surge`,`Daily energy`,`Battery capacity`,`Peak sun hours`],answer:0,explain:`Energy sizes the battery and array; power sizes the inverter.`}],summary:[`Record power, quantity, duty, hours and starting power for every load.`,`E = P × qty × duty × hours; E_d = E_AC/η + E_DC.`,`Peak = largest simultaneous running power; surge adds the largest start.`,`Inverter ≥ 1.25 × peak, surge capability ≥ surge.`,`Choose 12/24/48 V to keep battery current reasonable.`],homework:{id:`S2.M3.L1.hw`,title:`Profile a small clinic`,brief:`Loads: 12 LED lamps 8 W for 8 h; vaccine fridge 60 W, duty 0.5, 24 h; 2 laptops 60 W for 8 h; steriliser 1000 W for 1 h. Inverter η 0.9.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Daily AC energy (Wh)?`,answer:3448,tol:.01,unit:`Wh`,explain:`LEDs 12 × 8 × 8 = 768; vaccine fridge 60 × 0.5 × 24 = 720; laptops 2 × 60 × 8 = 960; steriliser 1000 × 1 = 1000; total 3448 Wh.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Energy at the battery (Wh)?`,answer:3831,tol:.01,unit:`Wh`,explain:`3448/0.9 = 3831 Wh.`}],written:[{prompt:`Explain why the clinic’s steriliser should run during the day.`,model:`Daytime energy can come directly from PV without passing through the battery (avoiding battery losses and cycling), and the steriliser’s high power then coincides with PV production; at night it would add 1 kWh of battery discharge and a large current peak.`}]}}},{id:`S2.M3.L2`,title:`Solar array sizing`,minutes:40,content:{objectives:[`Calculate the array power required to replace the daily energy in the design month.`,`Apply temperature, soiling, wiring, mismatch, controller and battery losses.`,`Convert the required power to a number of modules and a string configuration.`,`Check the configuration against the controller’s voltage and current limits.`],introduction:`The array must replace each day's energy in the design month — usually the month with the least sun for the loads it serves. This lesson turns daily energy into kilowatts of modules, accounting honestly for every loss on the way from sunlight to the battery. The method is exactly the one the laboratory shows on its Design tab.`,lecture:`### The design month and peak sun hours

Solar energy varies through the year. An off-grid system is sized for the **design month**: the one with the lowest ratio of sunlight to load (often the winter month, or the rainy season in the tropics). Its daily irradiation, in kWh/m²/day, equals the [[psh|peak sun hours]].

### Losses from module to battery

1. **Temperature:** k_T = 1 + γ(T_cell − 25), using the hot cell temperature.
2. **Soiling:** typical 0.95–0.98 (dust).
3. **DC wiring:** typical 0.97–0.99.
4. **Mismatch:** typical 0.98.
5. **Controller efficiency:** MPPT about 0.95–0.98.
6. **Battery round-trip efficiency:** lead-acid about 0.8–0.85, LiFePO₄ about 0.92–0.97 (typical values; use the datasheet).

The first four combine into the array derating factor k.

### Required array power

**P_array = E_d / (η_battery × η_controller × PSH × k)**

For a PWM controller, also divide by the PWM harvest factor (the fraction of MPP power available at battery voltage, lesson S2.M2.L2).

For a grid-tied system there is no battery: P_array = E_AC × offset / (PSH × k), where the offset is the fraction of the load to be produced on average.

### Modules and strings

- N = ⌈P_array / P_max⌉;
- choose the string length within the voltage window (lesson S2.M2.L1);
- strings = ⌈N / n⌉; installed modules = n × strings (often a little more than N).

### The controller

- MPPT output current ≥ P_installed × η / V_sys, next standard size;
- PV input voltage limit ≥ the cold string V_oc.

### Margins and judgement

The result is an estimate. Designers often add a margin for future loads or degradation, but every margin costs money: state it explicitly, as the laboratory does with its "assumption" labels.`,technical:`**Why the battery efficiency appears.** Off-grid energy used at night passes through the battery twice (charge and discharge). The method applies the round-trip efficiency to all of E_d, which is conservative for loads that run in daylight directly from PV. The 24-hour simulation (S2.M4) shows how much actually passes through the battery.

**Tilt and orientation.** Peak sun hours must be for the array's plane (tilt and azimuth), not the horizontal. Climate databases and design tools provide plane-of-array values; this course gives PSH directly.

**Cross-check.** E_produced = P_installed × PSH × k × η_controller should be at least E_d / η_battery. The laboratory's energy tab is a further check over several days.`,diagrams:[{id:`energy-balance`,caption:`PV production against the load: midday surplus charges the battery for the evening and night.`}],formulas:[{name:`Array derating factor`,expr:`k = [1 + γ(T_cell − 25)] × k_soiling × k_wiring × k_mismatch`,where:[]},{name:`Required array power (off-grid, MPPT)`,expr:`P_array = E_d / (η_bat · η_ctrl · PSH · k)`,where:[[`E_d`,`daily energy at the battery`,`Wh`],[`PSH`,`design-month peak sun hours`,`h`]]},{name:`Number of modules`,expr:`N = ⌈P_array / P_max⌉`,where:[]}],examples:[{title:`Array for the rural home`,problem:`E_d = 4002 Wh; illustrative 400 W modules (γ −0.35 %/°C, NOCT 45 °C); hottest 35 °C; PSH 4.5; soiling 0.95, wiring 0.97, mismatch 0.98; MPPT 0.97; LiFePO₄ 0.95.`,steps:[`T_cell = 35 + 25 = 60 °C; k_T = 1 − 0.0035 × 35 = 0.8775`,`k = 0.8775 × 0.95 × 0.97 × 0.98 = 0.7925`,`P_array = 4002 / (0.95 × 0.97 × 4.5 × 0.7925) = 4002 / 3.286 = 1218 W`,`N = ⌈1218 / 400⌉ = 4 modules`],answer:`1.22 kW required: four 400 W modules (1.6 kWp installed).`},{title:`Strings and controller`,problem:`For those modules: cold V_oc 38.40 V (12 °C), hot V_mp 28.07 V; 150 V MPPT; 24 V battery charging at 26.6 V. Configure the array and size the controller (sizes 40, 50, 60, 70, 80 A).`,steps:[`Window: n_max = ⌊150/38.40⌋ = 3; n_min = ⌈31.6/28.07⌉ = 2`,`Four modules: 2 × 2 uses exactly four (3 × 2 would need six)`,`I = 1600 × 0.97 / 24 = 64.7 A → 70 A`],answer:`Two strings of two; a 150 V, 70 A MPPT controller. Cold string V_oc 76.8 V.`}],applications:`The laboratory’s Design tab performs this method for any profile and shows the working; its energy tab then tests the result through a sequence of good and poor days.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Using annual-average peak sun hours for an off-grid system.`,correction:`Use the design month, or the system will fail in the poorest season.`},{mistake:`Leaving out the battery and controller efficiencies.`,correction:`Include every loss between the array and the loads.`},{mistake:`Rounding modules down.`,correction:`Round up, then fit the string window.`}],demo:{title:`Change the site and watch the array`,text:`On the Design tab, change the peak sun hours and the hottest temperature and watch the derating factor, the required array power and the string configuration update.`,link:e.solar(`Solar laboratory: array sizing`,`design`),embed:`array-config`},activities:[e.solar(`Solar laboratory: system design`,`design`),e.solar(`Energy management: test the design`,`energy`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Derating factor: k_T 0.9, soiling 0.95, wiring 0.98, mismatch 0.98?`,answer:.8211,tol:.01,unit:``,explain:`0.9 × 0.95 × 0.98 × 0.98.`},{id:`k2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`P_array for E_d 3 kWh, η_bat 0.85, η_ctrl 0.97, PSH 5, k 0.8 (W)?`,answer:909.6,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`3000/(0.85 × 0.97 × 5 × 0.8) = 909.6 W.`},{id:`k3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Modules of 330 W for 1.9 kW?`,answer:6,tol:.001,unit:``,explain:`1900/330 = 5.76 → 6.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`An off-grid array is sized for…`,options:[`the design month with the least sun for the load`,`the sunniest month`,`the annual average`,`night-time`],answer:0,explain:`It must work in the poorest season.`}],summary:[`Use the design month’s plane-of-array peak sun hours.`,`k = temperature × soiling × wiring × mismatch.`,`P_array = E_d / (η_bat · η_ctrl · PSH · k); PWM also ÷ harvest factor.`,`N = ⌈P/P_max⌉, then fit the string window.`,`Controller: I ≥ P·η/V_sys; V_max ≥ cold string V_oc.`],homework:{id:`S2.M3.L2.hw`,title:`Array sizing`,brief:`E_d = 6.2 kWh/day; illustrative 550 W modules (γ −0.35 %/°C, NOCT 45 °C); hottest 35 °C; PSH 4.5; soiling 0.95, wiring 0.97, mismatch 0.98; MPPT 0.97; lead-acid 0.85.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Derating factor k?`,answer:.7925,tol:.01,unit:``,explain:`0.8775 × 0.9031.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Required array power (W)?`,answer:2109,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`6200/(0.85 × 0.97 × 4.5 × 0.7925) = 6200/2.940 = 2109 W.`},{id:`h3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Number of modules?`,answer:4,tol:.001,unit:``,explain:`2109/550 = 3.83 → 4.`}],written:[{prompt:`The same clinic is moved to a site with PSH 3.2 in the rainy season. What changes, and what alternative to a larger array is worth considering?`,model:`The required array rises in proportion (×4.5/3.2 = 1.41) to about 2.97 kW: six modules. Alternatives: reduce or shift loads, accept generator back-up during the rainy season (hybrid off-grid design), or increase autonomy; the trade-off is cost against reliability.`}]}}},{id:`S2.M3.L3`,title:`Battery bank sizing`,minutes:40,content:{objectives:[`Calculate the battery energy and capacity for a required autonomy and depth of discharge.`,`Configure batteries in series and parallel for the system voltage and capacity.`,`Check discharge rate, charge current and the number of parallel strings.`,`Size the battery cable, fuse and isolator.`],introduction:`The battery carries the loads through the night and through cloudy days. Its size is a trade-off between reliability, cost and battery life: too small and it is cycled deeply and fails early; too large and it costs more than it saves. This lesson completes the design with the battery bank and its DC protection.`,lecture:`### Energy and capacity

**E_battery = E_d × autonomy / DoD**

- **[[autonomy|Autonomy]]:** days the battery must supply the loads without charging. Typical choices are 1–3 days for homes, more for critical loads in cloudy climates; less where a generator or the grid backs up the system.
- **[[dod|Depth of discharge]]:** the fraction of capacity used in normal cycling. Typical design values: about 50 % for lead-acid, about 80 % for LiFePO₄ (the datasheet's cycle-life curve sets the real value).

**C = E_battery / V_sys** (in Ah at the system voltage).

### Configuration

- Batteries in series per string: N_s = V_sys / V_unit (for example 2 × 12.8 V for 24 V, 4 × 12 V for 48 V).
- Parallel strings: N_p = ⌈C / C_unit⌉.
- Keep N_p small (ideally 1–3): more parallel strings share current unevenly and each needs a fuse. Prefer larger-capacity batteries or cells.
- Use identical batteries of the same type, age and state of charge; never mix chemistries.

### Discharge and charge rates

- Maximum discharge current: I_max = P_inverter / (η × V_cut-off).
- C-rate = I_max / C_bank. Check it against the datasheet. As typical guidance, lead-acid prefers about 0.2C or less; LiFePO₄ often allows 0.5C continuous or more, limited by its BMS.
- Charge current from the controller and any generator charger must also be within the datasheet limits.

### DC protection and cabling

From F1.M4 and F2.M5:

- **Battery fuse** at the battery positive: I_n ≥ 1.25 × I_max, DC-rated, with a breaking capacity above the bank's short-circuit current.
- **Fuse on each parallel string** when N_p > 1.
- **Cable** sized for the voltage-drop limit (often 1–2 % between battery and inverter) and checked for current-carrying capacity against the fuse rating.
- **Lockable battery isolator** for safe isolation.

### Hybrid systems

For hybrid systems the "autonomy" is the storage wanted for self-consumption and outages. A fraction of a day's energy is common; an outage reserve (for example 30 % SOC) is kept for back-up.`,technical:`**Temperature.** Lead-acid capacity falls in the cold (often 10–20 % at 0 °C); some designs add a temperature factor. Lithium batteries must not be charged below the minimum temperature in their datasheet, which their BMS enforces.

**Usable versus nominal.** A 25.6 V, 600 Ah LiFePO₄ bank is 15.36 kWh nominal and about 12.3 kWh usable at 80 % DoD. Always state which one a number refers to.

**System voltage versus bank voltage.** A "24 V" LiFePO₄ bank is nominally 25.6 V (8 cells). The method uses the system voltage (24 V) for the capacity in Ah, which is slightly conservative.`,diagrams:[{id:`battery-model`,caption:`The bank as an EMF with internal resistance: current, voltage sag and losses set the cable, fuse and C-rate checks.`}],formulas:[{name:`Battery energy`,expr:`E_bat = E_d × autonomy / DoD`,where:[]},{name:`Capacity and configuration`,expr:`C = E_bat / V_sys;  N_s = V_sys / V_unit;  N_p = ⌈C / C_unit⌉`,where:[]},{name:`Maximum discharge current`,expr:`I_max = P_inv / (η · V_cut-off)`,where:[[`V_cut-off`,`low-voltage cut-off (≈ 0.875 × nominal)`,`V`]]}],examples:[{title:`Bank for the rural home`,problem:`E_d = 4002 Wh, 2 days of autonomy, LiFePO₄ at 80 % DoD, 24 V system, 12.8 V 200 Ah batteries.`,steps:[`E_bat = 4002 × 2 / 0.8 = 10 006 Wh`,`C = 10 006 / 24 = 417 Ah`,`N_s = 24 / 12.8 → 2 (25.6 V); N_p = ⌈417 / 200⌉ = 3`],answer:`Two in series, three strings in parallel: six batteries, 25.6 V, 600 Ah, 15.36 kWh nominal.`},{title:`Current, fuse and cable`,problem:`The 1500 W inverter (η 0.9) cuts off at 21 V. Cable route 2 m; 2 % drop limit at 24 V; fuse sizes 80, 100, 125 A; cable sizes 16, 25, 35 mm².`,steps:[`I_max = 1500 / (0.9 × 21) = 79.4 A; C-rate = 79.4 / 600 = 0.13C`,`Fuse ≥ 1.25 × 79.4 = 99.2 A → 100 A`,`A ≥ 0.0172 × 4 × 79.4 / 0.48 = 11.4 mm² → 16 mm² (check its current rating ≥ 100 A in the manufacturer’s table; otherwise 25 mm²)`],answer:`100 A fuse; 16 mm² meets the drop limit if its rating is at least 100 A, otherwise 25 mm². Plus a fuse on each of the three strings rated at least 1.25 × 79.4 / 3 = 33 A (next standard size, 40 A).`}],applications:`The laboratory's Design tab sizes the bank and its protection, and flags excessive C-rates and too many parallel strings; the energy tab shows the state of charge over several days.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Sizing the battery for one day with lead-acid at 100 % DoD.`,correction:`Allow for autonomy and the allowed depth of discharge.`},{mistake:`Paralleling many small batteries.`,correction:`Keep strings few; use larger units and fuse each string.`},{mistake:`Forgetting the battery fuse’s breaking capacity.`,correction:`It must interrupt the bank’s short-circuit current at the DC voltage.`}],demo:{title:`Battery under load`,text:`Vary the load, internal resistance and capacity to see terminal voltage sag, internal heat and runtime; then size the bank on the Design tab.`,link:e.solar(`Solar laboratory: battery bank`,`design`),embed:`battery-load`},activities:[e.solar(`Solar laboratory: battery and protection`,`design`),e.lib(`Battery terminals`,`battery-terminals`),e.lib(`Fuse`,`fuse`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`E_bat for E_d 2.5 kWh, 3 days, DoD 0.5 (kWh)?`,answer:15,tol:.01,unit:`kWh`,explain:`2.5 × 3 / 0.5.`},{id:`k2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Capacity at 48 V for 15 kWh (Ah)?`,answer:312.5,tol:.01,unit:`Ah`,explain:`15 000/48.`},{id:`k3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Parallel strings of 200 Ah batteries for 312.5 Ah?`,answer:2,tol:.001,unit:``,explain:`⌈1.56⌉ = 2.`},{id:`k4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`I_max: 3 kW inverter, η 0.92, cut-off 42 V (A)?`,answer:77.64,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`3000/(0.92 × 42) = 77.6 A.`}],summary:[`E_bat = E_d × autonomy / DoD; C = E_bat / V_sys.`,`N_s = V_sys/V_unit; N_p = ⌈C/C_unit⌉; keep N_p small and fuse each string.`,`Check C-rate and charge current against the datasheet.`,`Battery fuse ≥ 1.25 × I_max with adequate breaking capacity; cable for drop and rating.`,`Hybrid storage: a fraction of daily energy plus an outage reserve.`],homework:{id:`S2.M3.L3.hw`,title:`Battery design for the clinic`,brief:`E_d = 6.2 kWh/day; 1.5 days of autonomy; lead-acid 12 V 200 Ah at 50 % DoD; 24 V; 2 kW inverter (η 0.9, cut-off 21 V).`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Battery energy (Wh)?`,answer:18600,tol:.01,unit:`Wh`,explain:`6200 × 1.5 / 0.5.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Capacity (Ah)?`,answer:775,tol:.01,unit:`Ah`,explain:`18 600/24.`},{id:`h3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Total batteries?`,answer:8,tol:.001,unit:``,explain:`2 in series × ⌈775/200⌉ = 4 strings = 8.`},{id:`h4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`C-rate at full inverter load?`,answer:.1323,tol:.02,unit:`C`,explain:`I = 2000/(0.9 × 21) = 105.8 A; 105.8/800 = 0.132C.`}],written:[{prompt:`Four parallel strings will be flagged. Suggest two ways to reduce them.`,model:`Use larger-capacity units (for example 2 V cells of 800 Ah, 12 in series, one string) or move to 48 V with fewer, larger batteries; alternatively reduce autonomy by adding generator back-up. Fewer strings share current evenly and need fewer fuses.`}]}}}],quiz:{pass:.7,questions:[{id:`q1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Daily energy: 6 LEDs 10 W × 6 h + TV 100 W × 4 h (Wh)?`,answer:760,tol:.01,unit:`Wh`,explain:`360 + 400.`},{id:`q2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Battery-side energy for 760 Wh at η 0.9 (Wh)?`,answer:844.4,tol:.01,unit:`Wh`,explain:`760/0.9.`},{id:`q3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Surge: peak 900 W, a 600 W pump starting at 1800 W (W)?`,answer:2100,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`900 + 1200.`},{id:`q4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`P_array: E_d 5 kWh, η_bat 0.95, η_ctrl 0.97, PSH 4, k 0.8 (W)?`,answer:1696,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`5000/2.948 = 1696 W.`},{id:`q5`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`E_bat: E_d 5 kWh, 2 days, DoD 0.8 (kWh)?`,answer:12.5,tol:.01,unit:`kWh`,explain:`5 × 2 / 0.8.`},{id:`q6`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`51.2 V, 100 Ah batteries for 12.5 kWh at 48 V?`,answer:3,tol:.001,unit:``,explain:`12 500/48 = 260 Ah → 3 in parallel.`},{id:`q7`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which value sizes the battery?`,options:[`Daily energy, autonomy and DoD`,`Peak power only`,`Inverter surge`,`Cable length`],answer:0,explain:`Energy storage.`},{id:`q8`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`An inverter of 4 kW on 12 V would draw roughly…`,options:[`about 420 A at cut-off: choose 48 V instead`,`40 A`,`100 A`,`10 A`],answer:0,explain:`4000/(0.9 × 10.5) = 423 A at cut-off: far too high for 12 V.`}]},assignment:{id:`S2.M3.asg`,title:`Module assignment: size a complete off-grid system by hand`,brief:`Use the rural home from S2.M3.L1 (E_d = 4002 Wh, peak 880 W, surge 2380 W). LiFePO₄ 12.8 V 200 Ah, DoD 0.8, η 0.95; 2 days autonomy; illustrative 400 W modules (cold V_oc 38.40 V, hot V_mp 28.07 V); PSH 4.5; k 0.7925; MPPT 0.97, 150 V.`,tasks:[{id:`a1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Inverter rating (1000/1500/2000 W, surge factor 2) (W)?`,answer:1500,tol:.001,unit:`W`,explain:`max(1100, 1190) → 1500 W.`},{id:`a2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Batteries in total?`,answer:6,tol:.001,unit:``,explain:`2S × 3P.`},{id:`a3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Required array power (W)?`,answer:1218,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`4002/(0.95 × 0.97 × 4.5 × 0.7925).`},{id:`a4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Controller output current at 24 V (A)?`,answer:64.67,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`1600 × 0.97/24 → 70 A controller.`},{id:`a5`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Battery fuse (A)?`,answer:100,tol:.001,unit:`A`,explain:`1.25 × 79.4 = 99.2 → 100 A.`}],written:[{prompt:`Enter the same home in the solar laboratory, compare its explained items with your answers, and run three days (100 %, 35 %, 80 % sun) on the energy tab. Report the lowest state of charge and any energy not supplied, and comment.`,model:`The laboratory reproduces the hand results (1500 W, 2S3P 15.36 kWh, 2 × 2 modules, 70 A, 100 A fuse). With 70 % starting SOC the bank stays above its 20 % limit through the poor day and recovers on the good days with no unmet energy; a run of several very poor days would eventually shed load, showing why autonomy and design-month sun hours matter.`}]},practicals:[{id:`S2.M3.P1`,title:`Solar laboratory: complete system design from a load profile`,description:`Design a complete system from a load profile in the solar laboratory with no design faults, and record it.`,link:e.solar(`Solar laboratory: Design tab`,`design`),evidence:{kind:`activity`,id:`solar:design-ok`}},{id:`S2.M3.P2`,title:`Hand-sizing assessment`,description:`Size a small off-grid system by hand. All answers must be correct.`,task:{id:`s2-sizing`,kind:`calc`,title:`Sizing an off-grid system`,brief:`Loads (AC, η_inv 0.9): 6 LED lamps 10 W for 5 h; a fridge 100 W with duty 0.5 for 24 h (starting 500 W); a TV 70 W for 4 h. Peak simultaneous load 230 W. 12 V LiFePO₄ 12.8 V 100 Ah batteries, DoD 0.8, η 0.95, 2 days autonomy. PSH 5; k 0.8; MPPT η 0.97. Inverter sizes 300, 500, 800 W; surge factor 2.`,questions:[{id:`c1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Daily AC energy (Wh)?`,answer:1780,tol:.01,unit:`Wh`,explain:`300 + 1200 + 280.`},{id:`c2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Energy at the battery (Wh)?`,answer:1977.8,tol:.01,unit:`Wh`,explain:`1780/0.9.`},{id:`c3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Inverter rating (W)?`,answer:500,tol:.001,unit:`W`,explain:`Continuous 1.25 × 230 = 288 W; surge 230 + 400 = 630 W → 315 W; → 500 W (300 W is below 315 W).`},{id:`c4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Battery capacity at 12 V (Ah)?`,answer:412,tol:.01,unit:`Ah`,explain:`1977.8 × 2 / 0.8 / 12 = 412 Ah.`},{id:`c5`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Number of 100 Ah batteries?`,answer:5,tol:.001,unit:``,explain:`⌈4.12⌉ = 5 in parallel (consider larger units).`},{id:`c6`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Required array power (W)?`,answer:536.7,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`1977.8/(0.95 × 0.97 × 5 × 0.8) = 536.6 W.`}],explain:`Energy sizes the battery and array; power and surge size the inverter. Every efficiency between the array and the loads is included.`},evidence:{kind:`task`,id:`s2-sizing`}}]},{id:`S2.M4`,title:`System types and energy management`,summary:`Off-grid, grid-connected and hybrid systems, generator integration, and the energy-management rules that decide where every watt-hour goes over a day — simulated in the laboratory’s 24-hour energy model.`,lessons:[{id:`S2.M4.L1`,title:`Off-grid systems`,minutes:35,content:{objectives:[`Describe the components and energy flows of an off-grid system.`,`Explain low-voltage disconnect, load shedding and curtailment.`,`Predict state of charge over a sequence of good and poor days.`,`Identify the design choices that decide reliability.`],introduction:`An off-grid system is the only source of electricity for its users: there is no grid to fall back on. Its battery must cover every night and every poor day, and its controls must protect that battery from being destroyed by over-discharge. This lesson follows energy through an off-grid system hour by hour.`,lecture:`### Components

**PV array → combiner and DC isolator → charge controller → battery (fuse, isolator) → inverter → AC distribution (protection, RCDs) → loads**

DC loads may be supplied directly from the battery or from the controller's load output.

### Energy flow through a day

- **Night:** the loads are supplied from the battery; state of charge ([[soc|SOC]]) falls.
- **Morning:** PV begins; first it supplies the loads, then any surplus charges the battery.
- **Midday:** PV usually exceeds the load; the battery charges (bulk, then absorption).
- **Battery full:** the controller reduces PV output to what the loads need. The energy the array could have produced is [[curtailment|curtailed]] — not stored and not used.
- **Evening:** PV falls below the load and the battery takes over again.

### Protecting the battery

- **Low-voltage disconnect ([[lvd|LVD]]):** the inverter (or controller load output) switches the loads off when the battery reaches its minimum SOC or voltage, set from the allowed depth of discharge. Loads are **shed** rather than destroying the battery.
- Some systems shed in stages: non-essential loads first, essential loads (vaccine fridge, lighting) last.

### Reliability

An off-grid system's reliability depends on:

- the design month's sun hours (worst-case season);
- autonomy (battery days);
- the ratio of array to load (array margin);
- load discipline (users shifting heavy loads to sunny hours).

A sequence of poor days drains the battery by the daily deficit each day; the battery recovers only on days when PV exceeds the load. The laboratory's energy tab simulates exactly this.`,technical:`**Daily deficit.** On a day producing a fraction w of the design-day PV energy, the battery's net change is roughly w × E_PV − E_d/η_bat. With E_PV sized to just replace E_d, a day at 35 % sun drains about 65 % of a day's energy from the battery; two days of autonomy therefore ride through about three such days.

**Inverter standby.** An inverter's idle consumption runs 24 hours: 15 W of idle is 360 Wh per day, a significant fraction of a small system's load. Load-sensing ("search") modes reduce it.

**The simulation.** The laboratory uses 10-minute steps, a clear-sky irradiance shape scaled to the site's peak sun hours and a day-by-day weather factor; battery efficiency is split between charge and discharge.`,diagrams:[{id:`energy-balance`,caption:`A day in an off-grid system: midday surplus charges the battery; evening and night loads discharge it.`},{id:`system-types`,caption:`The off-grid architecture alongside grid-tied and hybrid.`}],formulas:[{name:`Battery energy change over a day`,expr:`ΔE ≈ w · E_PV · η_charge − E_d`,where:[[`w`,`weather factor of the day (1 = design day)`,`—`]]},{name:`Minimum state of charge`,expr:`SOC_min = 1 − DoD`,where:[]}],examples:[{title:`A poor day`,problem:`A system stores 15.36 kWh nominal (80 % DoD, so 12.29 kWh usable) and needs 4.0 kWh/day. Its PV delivers about 4.4 kWh to the battery on a design day. Estimate the usable energy left after three days at 35 % sun, starting from full.`,steps:[`Daily net: 0.35 × 4.4 − 4.0 = 1.54 − 4.0 = −2.46 kWh`,`After three days: 12.29 − 3 × 2.46 = 4.91 kWh usable remains`],answer:`About 4.9 kWh of usable energy left: the system rides through.`},{title:`Curtailment`,problem:`The same battery is full at 11:00 on a sunny day. The array could produce 1.2 kW until 14:00 but the loads draw 0.3 kW. How much energy is curtailed?`,steps:[`Surplus = 1.2 − 0.3 = 0.9 kW`,`Curtailed ≈ 0.9 × 3 h = 2.7 kWh`],answer:`About 2.7 kWh is not harvested: a reason to schedule pumps or washing at midday.`}],applications:`Rural homes, clinics, schools, telecom sites and water pumping. The laboratory's off-grid examples and the clinic generator scenario are this kind of system.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Running heavy loads in the evening from the battery.`,correction:`Shift them to sunny hours when surplus PV would otherwise be curtailed.`},{mistake:`Setting the low-voltage disconnect below the battery’s allowed DoD.`,correction:`Set it from the datasheet’s recommended minimum.`},{mistake:`Ignoring inverter standby consumption.`,correction:`Include it in the daily energy, or use a search mode.`}],demo:{title:`Off-grid day`,text:`Select the off-grid home, change the weather and starting state of charge, and watch PV, load and SOC over 24 hours.`,link:e.solar(`Solar laboratory: energy management`,`energy`),embed:`energy-day`},activities:[e.solar(`Energy management: three-day scenario`,`energy`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Minimum SOC for DoD 0.5 (%)?`,answer:50,tol:.01,unit:`%`,explain:`1 − 0.5.`},{id:`k2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Daily net with E_PV 5 kWh at 40 % sun and E_d 4.5 kWh (kWh)?`,answer:-2.5,tol:.01,unit:`kWh`,explain:`2 − 4.5.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`When the battery is full and the loads are small, surplus PV is…`,options:[`curtailed by the controller`,`stored anyway`,`exported to the grid`,`sent to earth`],answer:0,explain:`Off-grid systems cannot export.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The low-voltage disconnect…`,options:[`sheds loads to protect the battery from over-discharge`,`charges the battery`,`protects the PV array`,`isolates the grid`],answer:0,explain:`LVD protects the battery.`}],summary:[`PV supplies loads first, then charges the battery; surplus is curtailed when full.`,`Nights and poor days discharge the battery; LVD sheds loads at the DoD limit.`,`Reliability: design month, autonomy, array margin and load discipline.`,`Shift heavy loads to sunny hours.`,`Include inverter standby consumption.`],homework:{id:`S2.M4.L1.hw`,title:`Riding through cloudy days`,brief:`Usable storage 8 kWh; E_d 3.2 kWh/day; design-day PV to the battery 3.6 kWh.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Net change on a 25 % sun day (kWh)?`,answer:-2.3,tol:.01,unit:`kWh`,explain:`0.9 − 3.2.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Whole days at 25 % sun before LVD, starting full?`,answer:3,tol:.001,unit:``,explain:`8/2.3 = 3.48 → 3 full days.`}],written:[{prompt:`Suggest two operational measures that improve this system’s reliability without new equipment.`,model:`Shift deferrable loads (pumping, washing, ironing) to sunny midday hours; shed non-essential loads early when SOC falls (staged LVD); keep modules clean; switch off the inverter or use search mode at night if no AC loads run.`}]}}},{id:`S2.M4.L2`,title:`Grid-connected systems`,minutes:35,content:{objectives:[`Describe the components and operation of a grid-tied PV system.`,`Calculate self-consumption, import and export over a day.`,`Explain anti-islanding behaviour and its safety purpose.`,`Estimate annual yield and size the inverter with a DC/AC ratio.`],introduction:`A [[grid-tied]] system uses the grid as its "battery": PV supplies the site's loads, surplus is exported, and shortfalls are imported. It is the simplest and cheapest way to use solar energy where a reliable grid exists — but it provides no power during an outage. This lesson explains why, and how to estimate what such a system delivers.`,lecture:`### Components

**PV array → DC isolator → grid inverter (MPPT + bridge) → AC isolator → consumer unit → meter → grid**

Protection includes AC overcurrent protection and the inverter's internal grid-protection functions; surge protection is often fitted on both sides.

### Energy flow

At every moment: **P_PV,AC = P_load + P_export − P_import**.

- When PV exceeds the load, the surplus is **exported** (where permitted, possibly limited by the network operator).
- When the load exceeds PV, the difference is **imported**.
- **Self-consumption** is the PV energy used on site; **self-sufficiency** is the fraction of the load supplied by PV.

### Anti-islanding

A grid inverter **must** stop exporting within a short time when the grid fails. If it did not, it could keep a section of the network energised — an "island" — while utility workers treat it as dead (back-feed, F1.M2.L3).

Consequences:

- a grid-tied system **gives no back-up**, even in full sun;
- back-up needs a battery and a hybrid or off-grid inverter with a transfer arrangement (lesson L3).

### Yield and sizing

- **Annual yield:** E_year ≈ P_array × PSH_annual × 365 × PR, where the performance ratio PR (≈ k × η_inverter) is typically 0.75–0.85.
- **Inverter:** P_AC ≈ P_array / DC/AC ratio (typically 1.1–1.3).
- **Strings:** within the inverter's MPPT voltage range and below its maximum DC voltage (S2.M2.L1).

### Connection rules

Connection to the grid requires the network operator's approval and settings (export limits, protection settings, power factor). Installation and certification follow the national rules (course S3).`,technical:`**Self-consumption depends on timing.** A home that uses most energy in the evening exports most of its midday PV. Shifting loads to daytime, or adding a battery (hybrid), raises self-consumption. The laboratory's grid-tied example shows the export peak at midday and imports in the evening.

**Export limiting.** Where export is not allowed, the inverter must curtail its output to the site's load, using a meter at the grid connection. The energy model represents this as curtailment.

**Annual versus design month.** Grid-tied systems are sized on annual energy, unlike off-grid systems sized on the worst month: the grid absorbs seasonal surpluses and deficits.`,diagrams:[{id:`system-types`,caption:`The grid-tied architecture: no battery, export and import through the meter.`}],formulas:[{name:`Power balance`,expr:`P_PV,AC = P_load + P_export − P_import`,where:[]},{name:`Annual yield`,expr:`E_year ≈ P_array × PSH × 365 × PR`,where:[[`PR`,`performance ratio ≈ k × η_inv`,`—`]]},{name:`Self-sufficiency`,expr:`S = 1 − E_import / E_load`,where:[]}],examples:[{title:`Annual yield`,problem:`A 4 kWp array at a site with 5.0 average peak sun hours and PR 0.8.`,steps:[`E_year = 4 × 5.0 × 365 × 0.8`,`E_year = 5840 kWh`],answer:`About 5.8 MWh per year.`},{title:`A day’s import and export`,problem:`A home uses 12 kWh/day: 4 kWh between 09:00 and 16:00 and 8 kWh at other times. The PV produces 14 kWh, all between 09:00 and 16:00. Estimate export, import and self-sufficiency.`,steps:[`Daytime: PV 14 kWh, load 4 kWh → export ≈ 10 kWh (ignoring short dips)`,`Night: import ≈ 8 kWh`,`Self-sufficiency = 1 − 8/12 = 33 %`],answer:`Export ≈ 10 kWh, import ≈ 8 kWh, self-sufficiency ≈ 33 %, although PV exceeds the daily load.`}],applications:`Rooftop systems on homes, businesses and public buildings wherever the grid is reliable and connection is permitted. The laboratory's “grid-tied home” example and its outage scenario illustrate both the energy flows and anti-islanding.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Promising back-up power from a grid-tied system.`,correction:`Anti-islanding stops it during outages; back-up needs storage.`},{mistake:`Equating daily PV energy greater than load with independence.`,correction:`Timing matters; evening loads are still imported.`},{mistake:`Connecting without the network operator’s approval.`,correction:`Grid connection requires approval and specified settings.`}],demo:{title:`Grid-tied day`,text:`Select the grid-tied example and watch export at midday and import in the evening; then simulate an outage on the energy tab.`,link:e.solar(`Solar laboratory: energy management with an outage`,`energy`),embed:`energy-day`},activities:[e.solar(`Energy management: grid outage`,`energy`),e.solar(`Troubleshooting: no power during an outage`,`faults`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Annual yield: 6 kWp, PSH 4.2, PR 0.78 (kWh)?`,answer:7174,tol:.01,unit:`kWh`,explain:`6 × 4.2 × 365 × 0.78 = 7174 kWh.`},{id:`k2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Self-sufficiency with 9 kWh imported of 15 kWh load (%)?`,answer:40,tol:.01,unit:`%`,explain:`1 − 0.6.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`During a grid outage at noon, a grid-tied system without battery…`,options:[`produces nothing`,`supplies the house`,`exports`,`charges the meter`],answer:0,explain:`Anti-islanding.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Grid-tied systems are usually sized on…`,options:[`annual energy`,`the worst month only`,`the peak load`,`battery autonomy`],answer:0,explain:`The grid balances the seasons.`}],summary:[`PV → grid inverter → site loads; surplus exported, deficit imported.`,`Self-consumption and self-sufficiency depend on timing.`,`Anti-islanding: no output during outages; protects line workers.`,`Yield ≈ P × PSH × 365 × PR; inverter ≈ P_array / 1.1–1.3.`,`Connection needs the network operator’s approval.`],homework:{id:`S2.M4.L2.hw`,title:`Grid-tied estimates`,brief:`A 5 kWp array, average PSH 4.6, PR 0.8; the home uses 6000 kWh/year.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Annual yield (kWh)?`,answer:6716,tol:.01,unit:`kWh`,explain:`5 × 4.6 × 365 × 0.8 = 6716 kWh.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Inverter AC rating at DC/AC 1.2 (kW)?`,answer:4.167,tol:.01,unit:`kW`,explain:`5/1.2.`}],written:[{prompt:`The owner says “the system makes more than we use, so we never need the grid”. Explain why this is wrong.`,model:`Annual totals hide timing: PV produces only in daylight and more in some seasons, while the home uses energy at night and every day; without storage the home imports in the evenings and at night and exports at midday, and during outages the inverter stops entirely (anti-islanding).`}]}}},{id:`S2.M4.L3`,title:`Hybrid systems and generator integration`,minutes:40,content:{objectives:[`Describe hybrid operating modes: self-consumption, back-up and grid charging.`,`Integrate a generator: sizing, charger current and automatic start and stop thresholds.`,`Explain transfer switching and why generator and grid must never be paralleled unintentionally.`,`Configure reserve and threshold settings and check them in simulation.`],introduction:`Hybrid systems combine PV, a battery and one or more AC sources — the grid, a generator, or both. They give the reliability of an off-grid system with the economy of grid connection, and allow a much smaller generator to run less often. Their behaviour is set by a few settings that must be chosen with care: the laboratory's clinic scenario shows what happens when one is wrong.`,lecture:`### Hybrid with the grid

Typical operating modes:

- **Self-consumption:** PV supplies the loads first, surplus charges the battery, then any remainder is exported (or curtailed). The battery discharges in the evening down to a **reserve** SOC.
- **Back-up:** the reserve is kept for outages. When the grid fails, the inverter isolates from the grid (anti-islanding) and supplies the backed-up loads from battery and PV.
- **Grid charging:** where useful (time-of-use tariffs, or before an expected outage), the battery is charged from the grid.

### Off-grid with a generator

The generator covers long poor-weather spells, allowing a smaller array and battery:

- **Auto-start** when the SOC falls below a threshold; **auto-stop** above a higher threshold. The gap (hysteresis) prevents frequent short runs.
- The **start threshold must be above the low-voltage disconnect**, or the loads will be shed before the generator starts.
- While running, the generator supplies the loads and the **charger**.

### Sizing the generator

- Charger current: typically about 0.1–0.2 C for lead-acid, or as the battery datasheet allows.
- Charger power: P_ch = I_ch × V_charge / η_charger.
- Generator: S ≥ 1.25 × (P_peak + P_ch) / PF, with PF 0.8 as the usual rating basis.

Running a generator at a reasonable load is also more fuel-efficient than running it lightly loaded.

### Transfer switching

Only one source may supply the AC loads at a time unless equipment is specifically designed to synchronise:

- a **[[transfer-switch|transfer switch]]** (manual or automatic, with interlocking) selects grid or generator;
- the inverter's internal transfer relay switches loads between the AC input (bypass) and its own output.

Connecting a generator to a circuit that is also connected to the grid back-feeds the network (F1.M2.L3) and can destroy the generator when the grid returns. Transfer arrangements are interlocked and installed by qualified persons.`,technical:`**Generator start logic in practice.** Controllers combine SOC with other conditions: a minimum run time, quiet hours, a load-power trigger (start if the load exceeds the inverter's rating for some time) and an exercise run. This course models the SOC thresholds, which are the essential design decision.

**The clinic example.** With a 50 % DoD lead-acid bank, a generator start at 20 % SOC can never trigger: the LVD sheds the loads at 50 %. Setting the start at 55 % and the stop at 90 % lets the generator run a few hours after poor days, as the energy tab shows.

**Build project H** implements a simplified hybrid inverter with a grid/generator selector relay and a transfer relay in the simulator.`,diagrams:[{id:`hybrid-paths`,caption:`Hybrid energy paths: grid or generator selection, transfer relay, charger, battery and inverter.`},{id:`system-types`,caption:`Hybrid architecture compared with off-grid and grid-tied.`}],formulas:[{name:`Charger power`,expr:`P_ch = I_ch × V_charge / η_ch`,where:[]},{name:`Generator rating`,expr:`S_gen ≥ 1.25 × (P_peak + P_ch) / PF`,where:[[`PF`,`rating power factor (0.8)`,`—`]]},{name:`Threshold rule`,expr:`SOC_stop > SOC_start > SOC_LVD = 1 − DoD`,where:[]}],examples:[{title:`Generator for the clinic`,problem:`Lead-acid bank 24 V, 800 Ah; charger current 0.15 C at 28.8 V, η 0.9; peak load 1310 W. Sizes 5, 6, 8, 10 kVA.`,steps:[`I_ch = 0.15 × 800 = 120 A; P_ch = 120 × 28.8 / 0.9 = 3840 W`,`S = 1.25 × (1310 + 3840) / 0.8 = 8047 VA`,`Next size: 10 kVA`],answer:`A 10 kVA generator; most of its load is battery charging.`},{title:`Checking the thresholds`,problem:`The bank’s DoD is 50 %. The installer set start 20 %, stop 80 %. What happens on a long cloudy spell, and what should be set?`,steps:[`LVD at 1 − 0.5 = 50 % SOC: loads are shed at 50 %`,`The SOC never reaches 20 %, so the generator never starts`,`Set start above 50 % (for example 55 %) and stop well above it (for example 90 %)`],answer:`The clinic loses power with a working generator; correct thresholds: start 55 %, stop 90 %.`}],applications:`Clinics, schools, farms and businesses on weak grids or with generator back-up; the laboratory's clinic and hybrid-home examples, the generator-threshold troubleshooting scenario and build project H.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Setting the generator start below the low-voltage disconnect.`,correction:`SOC_start must be above SOC_LVD.`},{mistake:`Sizing the generator for the loads only.`,correction:`Include the charger’s power.`},{mistake:`Connecting a generator without an interlocked transfer arrangement.`,correction:`It can back-feed the grid; use a proper transfer switch installed by a qualified person.`}],demo:{title:`Hybrid and generator energy flows`,text:`Run the clinic example over three poor days and watch the generator start and stop; then try the hybrid home with a grid outage.`,link:e.solar(`Solar laboratory: energy management`,`energy`),embed:`energy-day`},activities:[e.build(`Build project H: hybrid inverter`,`bld-h`),e.solar(`Troubleshooting: generator does not start`,`faults`),e.solar(`Energy management: generator scenario`,`energy`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Charger power: 60 A at 57.6 V, η 0.9 (W)?`,answer:3840,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`60 × 57.6 / 0.9.`},{id:`k2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Generator for P_peak 2 kW and P_ch 3.84 kW (kVA)?`,answer:9.125,tol:.01,unit:`kVA`,explain:`1.25 × 5840 / 0.8 = 9125 VA.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`LiFePO₄ at 80 % DoD. Which start/stop thresholds work?`,options:[`start 30 %, stop 85 %`,`start 15 %, stop 80 %`,`start 50 %, stop 40 %`,`start 10 %, stop 20 %`],answer:0,explain:`LVD at 20 %; start must be above it and stop above start.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`In a hybrid system the outage reserve…`,options:[`is battery energy kept for grid failures`,`is exported first`,`is the generator fuel`,`is the PV curtailment`],answer:0,explain:`Self-consumption stops at the reserve while the grid is up.`}],summary:[`Hybrid modes: self-consumption to a reserve, back-up on outage, optional grid charging.`,`Generator: start/stop on SOC with hysteresis; start above the LVD.`,`S_gen ≥ 1.25 × (P_peak + P_charger)/0.8.`,`One AC source at a time: interlocked transfer switching.`,`Check settings in simulation before commissioning.`],homework:{id:`S2.M4.L3.hw`,title:`Generator integration`,brief:`48 V lead-acid bank of 400 Ah (DoD 0.5), charger 0.15 C at 57.6 V (η 0.9), peak load 2.5 kW; sizes 5, 6, 8, 10 kVA.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Charger current (A)?`,answer:60,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`0.15 × 400.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Required generator rating (kVA)?`,answer:9.906,tol:.01,unit:`kVA`,explain:`P_ch = 3840 W; 1.25 × 6340 / 0.8 = 9906 VA → 10 kVA.`},{id:`h3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Suitable thresholds?`,options:[`start 55 %, stop 90 %`,`start 40 %, stop 90 %`,`start 55 %, stop 50 %`,`start 20 %, stop 95 %`],answer:0,explain:`LVD 50 %: start above it, stop well above start.`}],written:[{prompt:`Explain why a large generator running for 2 hours is often better than a small one running for 8.`,model:`Generators are most efficient near their rated load; a larger unit can supply the loads and a high charging current together, so the battery recharges in fewer, well-loaded hours, reducing fuel per kWh, run hours, noise and maintenance (within the battery’s charge-current limits).`}]}}},{id:`S2.M4.L4`,title:`Energy management`,minutes:40,content:{objectives:[`State the dispatch priorities of off-grid, grid-tied and hybrid systems.`,`Run multi-day simulations and interpret PV, load, SOC, import, export, generator and unmet energy.`,`Evaluate a design against weather sequences and outages.`,`Propose changes to settings, loads or equipment from simulation results.`],introduction:`Energy management is the set of rules that decides, moment by moment, where energy comes from and where it goes. Good rules make a modest system perform well; poor rules waste energy or leave users in the dark. This lesson brings the module together in the laboratory's multi-day energy simulation, and teaches you to read it like an engineer.`,lecture:`### Dispatch priorities

**Off-grid**

1. PV supplies the loads.
2. Surplus PV charges the battery (up to the controller's current limit).
3. Remaining surplus is curtailed.
4. Shortfalls come from the battery down to its DoD limit.
5. If a generator is configured and SOC is below the start threshold, it supplies the loads and charges until the stop threshold.
6. Anything still unsupplied is **unmet** (loads shed).

**Grid-tied**

1. PV supplies the loads.
2. Surplus is exported (if permitted) or curtailed.
3. Shortfalls are imported.
4. During an outage: no PV output; loads unsupplied.

**Hybrid**

1. PV supplies the loads, then charges the battery, then exports (if permitted).
2. While the grid is up, the battery discharges only down to the reserve; then the grid supplies the loads.
3. During an outage, the battery (down to its DoD limit) and PV supply the backed-up loads.

### Reading a simulation

- **SOC curve:** does it recover each sunny day? How low does it go after poor days?
- **Unmet energy:** the reliability indicator for off-grid systems. Zero on design days is the minimum requirement.
- **Curtailment:** energy wasted when the battery is full; high curtailment suggests moving loads to daytime.
- **Import/export:** for grid-connected systems, the economics.
- **Generator hours and starts:** fuel, noise and maintenance.

### Improving a design

| Symptom in the simulation | Possible changes |
|---|---|
| Unmet load after two poor days | More autonomy, more PV, generator back-up, load reduction |
| SOC never recovers fully | Array too small for the design month |
| Large midday curtailment | Shift loads to daytime; smaller battery may suffice |
| Frequent short generator runs | Wider start/stop hysteresis |
| Large evening import (hybrid) | Larger battery or lower reserve |

### Limits of the model

The simulation uses clear-sky irradiance scaled by a daily weather factor, typical efficiencies and a fixed hourly load profile. Real weather is irregular, loads vary and batteries age. Simulation supports judgement; it does not replace monitoring of the real system.`,technical:`**Time step.** The model uses 10-minute steps. Charging power is limited by the controller rating; the battery's efficiency is split equally between charge and discharge (√η each way), so a full round trip loses η.

**Self-sufficiency** is reported as 1 − (grid import + generator energy + unmet)/load. Generator energy that charges the battery is counted against self-sufficiency on the day it runs.

**Monitoring.** Real systems log the same quantities. Comparing logged data with a simulation of the design is how degraded modules, a weak battery or a mis-set threshold are found (S2.M5.L2).`,diagrams:[{id:`energy-balance`,caption:`PV against the load: the shape of both curves decides how much energy passes through the battery.`}],formulas:[{name:`Energy balance over a period`,expr:`E_PV + E_import + E_gen = E_load,served + E_export + E_curtailed + ΔE_battery + losses`,where:[]},{name:`Self-sufficiency`,expr:`S = 1 − (E_import + E_gen + E_unmet) / E_load`,where:[]}],examples:[{title:`Reading an off-grid run`,problem:`A three-day run (100 %, 35 %, 80 % sun) shows: SOC 70 % → 100 % (day 1) → 41 % (morning of day 3) → 92 % (end); unmet 0; curtailed 3.1 kWh on day 1. Is the design acceptable and what would you suggest?`,steps:[`The SOC stays above the 20 % limit and recovers: reliable for this sequence`,`Curtailment on the sunny day shows spare midday energy`,`Suggest scheduling pumping and washing for sunny middays; test a longer poor spell`],answer:`Acceptable; use the surplus by shifting loads, and test a worse sequence before relying on it.`},{title:`A hybrid outage`,problem:`A hybrid home with a 10.24 kWh battery (reserve 30 %, DoD 80 %) loses the grid at 18:00 for 6 hours with SOC 60 %. The backed-up evening load averages 600 W. Will the battery cover the outage?`,steps:[`Energy needed ≈ 0.6 kW × 6 h / 0.9 (inverter) ≈ 4.0 kWh`,`Available from 60 % down to the 20 % DoD limit = 0.4 × 10.24 = 4.1 kWh`,`4.1 ≥ 4.0: just enough`],answer:`Just covered; a higher reserve or a larger battery would give margin.`}],applications:`Every system design is checked in the energy simulation before it is considered complete; the S2 practical requires a recorded simulation. Operators use the same reasoning to set reserves, thresholds and load schedules.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Judging a design from a single sunny day.`,correction:`Test sequences of poor days and, for hybrids, outages.`},{mistake:`Ignoring curtailment.`,correction:`It is wasted energy that load shifting can recover.`},{mistake:`Treating simulation results as guarantees.`,correction:`They are estimates under assumed weather and loads; monitor the real system.`}],demo:{title:`Energy management simulation`,text:`Pick a system, set three days of weather and the starting state of charge, and read every energy flow and total.`,link:e.solar(`Solar laboratory: energy management`,`energy`),embed:`energy-day`},activities:[e.solar(`Energy management: run and record a simulation`,`energy`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Self-sufficiency: load 30 kWh, import 6, generator 0, unmet 0 (%)?`,answer:80,tol:.01,unit:`%`,explain:`1 − 6/30.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Off-grid simulation shows unmet energy on day 3 of a cloudy spell. A first remedy to consider is…`,options:[`more autonomy, more PV, a generator or fewer loads`,`a larger inverter`,`longer cables`,`a lower DoD limit`],answer:0,explain:`Energy, not power, is short.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`In a hybrid with the grid up, the battery discharges…`,options:[`down to the reserve, then the grid supplies the loads`,`to empty`,`never`,`only at noon`],answer:0,explain:`The reserve is kept for outages.`},{id:`k4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Usable energy from 70 % to 20 % of 12 kWh (kWh)?`,answer:6,tol:.01,unit:`kWh`,explain:`0.5 × 12.`}],summary:[`Off-grid: PV → loads → battery → curtail; then battery → generator → unmet.`,`Grid-tied: PV → loads → export; import shortfalls; nothing in outages.`,`Hybrid: self-consumption to a reserve; back-up from the battery in outages.`,`Read SOC, unmet, curtailment, import/export and generator hours.`,`Test poor-weather sequences and outages; simulations are estimates.`],homework:{id:`S2.M4.L4.hw`,title:`Evaluate a design`,brief:`Run the “Off-grid rural home” example in the laboratory for three days with 100 %, 20 % and 20 % sun, starting at 70 % SOC.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`With two days of autonomy, would you expect load to be shed by the end of day 3?`,options:[`Possibly: two very poor days after only a partial recharge can approach the DoD limit; run it and see`,`Never`,`Always on day 1`,`Only if the inverter is too small`],answer:0,explain:`Each 20 % day drains about 80 % of a day’s energy.`}],written:[{prompt:`Report the minimum SOC and any unmet energy from your run, and propose one change to the design or its operation, justifying it from the results.`,model:`A sample answer reports the lowest SOC and unmet kWh read from the energy tab; if load was shed, a justified change is adding autonomy (a third battery string) or a generator with a start threshold above the LVD; if not, shifting the pump to midday to use curtailed energy.`}]}}}],quiz:{pass:.7,questions:[{id:`q1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`In an off-grid system, when the battery is full the controller…`,options:[`curtails PV to what the loads need`,`exports`,`disconnects the loads`,`starts the generator`],answer:0,explain:`No export path.`},{id:`q2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Annual yield: 3 kWp, PSH 5.5, PR 0.8 (kWh)?`,answer:4818,tol:.01,unit:`kWh`,explain:`3 × 5.5 × 365 × 0.8.`},{id:`q3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A generator start threshold must be…`,options:[`above the low-voltage disconnect level`,`below it`,`equal to the stop threshold`,`zero`],answer:0,explain:`Otherwise loads are shed first.`},{id:`q4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Generator for P_peak 1.5 kW, P_ch 2.5 kW (kVA)?`,answer:6.25,tol:.01,unit:`kVA`,explain:`1.25 × 4000 / 0.8.`},{id:`q5`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Anti-islanding exists to…`,options:[`protect people working on lines believed dead`,`save battery energy`,`improve power factor`,`reduce curtailment`],answer:0,explain:`Safety of line workers.`},{id:`q6`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Daily net with E_PV 6 kWh at 30 % sun and E_d 5 kWh (kWh)?`,answer:-3.2,tol:.01,unit:`kWh`,explain:`1.8 − 5.`},{id:`q7`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Large curtailment in an off-grid simulation suggests…`,options:[`shifting loads to sunny hours`,`adding more modules`,`raising the DoD`,`removing the battery`],answer:0,explain:`Use the surplus.`},{id:`q8`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A transfer switch ensures…`,options:[`only one AC source supplies the loads at a time`,`the battery charges faster`,`PV is always exported`,`the generator runs continuously`],answer:0,explain:`Prevents paralleling grid and generator.`}]},assignment:{id:`S2.M4.asg`,title:`Module assignment: energy-management study of the clinic`,brief:`Use the “Rural clinic with generator back-up” example in the solar laboratory (lead-acid 24 V, 800 Ah, DoD 0.5, generator 10 kVA, start 55 %, stop 90 %).`,tasks:[{id:`a1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Usable battery energy at 24 V (kWh)?`,answer:9.6,tol:.01,unit:`kWh`,explain:`24 × 800 × 0.5 = 9.6 kWh.`},{id:`a2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Battery energy between start (55 %) and LVD (50 %) (kWh)?`,answer:.96,tol:.01,unit:`kWh`,explain:`0.05 × 19.2.`},{id:`a3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`At an average evening load of 400 W from the battery, about how long between generator start and LVD?`,options:[`About 2 hours`,`About 10 minutes`,`About 24 hours`,`It never reaches LVD`],answer:0,explain:`0.96 kWh / ~0.44 kW ≈ 2 h; the generator starts at 55 %, so it normally prevents LVD.`}],written:[{prompt:`Run three days at 30 % sun from 60 % SOC, report generator starts, hours and unmet energy, and then repeat with start 20 %. Explain the difference.`,model:`With start 55 %, the generator starts once or twice, runs a few hours each time and no load is shed. With start 20 %, it never starts because the LVD disconnects the loads at 50 %: several kWh are not supplied. The start threshold must be above the LVD.`}]},practicals:[{id:`S2.M4.P1`,title:`Solar laboratory: multi-day energy-management simulation`,description:`Run and record a multi-day energy simulation of a design and interpret its results.`,link:e.solar(`Energy management`,`energy`),evidence:{kind:`activity`,id:`solar:sim`}},{id:`S2.M4.P2`,title:`Build project H: hybrid inverter`,description:`Design, assemble and test the hybrid inverter with grid/generator selection and transfer relay in the build laboratory.`,link:e.build(`Build project H`,`bld-h`),evidence:{kind:`project`,id:`bld-h`}},{id:`S2.M4.P3`,title:`Generator integration assessment`,description:`Size a generator and set its thresholds. All answers must be correct.`,task:{id:`s2-generator`,kind:`calc`,title:`Generator back-up`,brief:`Off-grid 48 V LiFePO₄ bank of 300 Ah, DoD 0.8; charger 0.2 C at 55.2 V, η 0.9; peak load 2.4 kW. Generator sizes 5, 6, 8, 10 kVA (PF 0.8 rating basis).`,questions:[{id:`c1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Charger current (A)?`,answer:60,tol:.001,unit:`A`,explain:`0.2 × 300.`},{id:`c2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Charger power (W)?`,answer:3680,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`60 × 55.2 / 0.9 = 3680 W.`},{id:`c3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Required generator rating (kVA)?`,answer:9.5,tol:.01,unit:`kVA`,explain:`1.25 × 6080 / 0.8 = 9500 VA.`},{id:`c4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Chosen size (kVA)?`,answer:10,tol:.001,unit:`kVA`,explain:`Next size.`},{id:`c5`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Low-voltage disconnect SOC (%)?`,answer:20,tol:.001,unit:`%`,explain:`1 − 0.8.`},{id:`c6`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Thresholds?`,options:[`start 30 %, stop 90 %`,`start 15 %, stop 90 %`,`start 30 %, stop 25 %`,`start 90 %, stop 30 %`],answer:0,explain:`Start above the LVD; stop above start.`}],explain:`The generator supplies the loads and the charger together with margin; the start threshold sits above the low-voltage disconnect and the stop threshold well above it.`},evidence:{kind:`task`,id:`s2-generator`}}]},{id:`S2.M5`,title:`Installation, commissioning and troubleshooting`,summary:`Turning a design into a safe installation: DC and AC protection, isolation and labelling; the commissioning sequence with predicted and measured values; and a systematic method for finding faults in solar and battery systems.`,lessons:[{id:`S2.M5.L1`,title:`Installation design and commissioning`,minutes:45,content:{objectives:[`List the protection, isolation, earthing and labelling an installation design must specify.`,`Describe the commissioning sequence for a PV and battery system.`,`Predict string V_oc and I_sc for the measurement conditions and judge measurements against them.`,`Produce a commissioning record and explain why it matters.`],introduction:`A design becomes an installation through careful specification and testing. [[commissioning|Commissioning]] is the structured process that proves the installation is safe and performs as designed before it is handed over — and it leaves a record that every later maintenance visit and fault-finding job depends on. In this lesson you will practise it in the laboratory with predicted and simulated measured values. Real installation and commissioning are carried out by qualified persons under the applicable standards (course S3 develops them).`,lecture:`### What the installation design specifies

**DC side (PV):**

- string configuration and cable type (solar cable, UV-resistant, double insulated) and cross-section;
- polarised connectors of one compatible type;
- string fuses where required, both poles where the standard requires;
- surge protection;
- a lockable **DC isolator** rated for the cold string V_oc and 1.25 × array I_sc.

**DC side (battery):**

- battery fuse at the positive terminal with adequate breaking capacity;
- fuses on parallel strings;
- battery isolator, cable sizes, terminal covers;
- ventilation where batteries can release gas.

**AC side:**

- overcurrent protection;
- RCDs where required;
- the earthing arrangement and bonding of frames (F1.M4.L2);
- transfer switching for generators and back-up.

**Labelling:** warnings that PV conductors stay live in daylight, isolation points, the DC voltage, the battery's stored energy and emergency shutdown instructions.

### The commissioning sequence

1. **Visual inspection** against the design: components, ratings, cable routes, connectors, fixings, labels.
2. **Before connection — DC tests on each string (isolators open):**
   - polarity;
   - open-circuit voltage V_oc;
   - short-circuit or operating current (with suitable instruments);
   - [[insulation-resistance|insulation resistance]] to earth.
3. **Battery:** bank voltage and polarity before connection; correct configuration.
4. **Settings:** charge set-points from the battery datasheet; generator thresholds; grid settings from the network operator.
5. **Energise in the manufacturer's order**; functional tests: inverter output voltage and frequency, load test, transfer to generator, generator auto-start, anti-islanding (for grid systems, where the procedure requires).
6. **Record** everything: values, instruments, conditions, settings, as-built drawing.

### Predicting what you should measure

Commissioning compares measurements with **expected values for the conditions** at the time of the test:

- **V_oc,string = n × [V_oc,STC(1 + β(T_cell − 25)) + a·ln(G/1000)]**
- **I_sc = I_sc,STC × G/1000 × (1 + α(T_cell − 25))**

Irradiance is measured with a reference cell in the array's plane and the module temperature with a probe on the back. Typical acceptance:

- V_oc within a few per cent of expected (this laboratory uses ±5 %), and strings within a small spread of each other;
- I_sc within about ±10 % (irradiance measurement is the main uncertainty).

The **insulation-resistance limit** depends on the system voltage and is given by the applicable standard (for PV, typically IEC 62446-1 or its national equivalent). Look it up: do not guess it.`,technical:`**What deviations mean.**

- One module's voltage missing from a string: a short-circuited or reversed module, or a bypass diode failed short.
- Negative V_oc: reversed string polarity. Never connect it.
- Low I_sc on all strings: irradiance measurement, soiling or shading.
- Low insulation resistance: damaged insulation, a pinched cable, water in a connector or junction box.

**Instruments.** Commissioning uses instruments rated for the DC voltage and measurement category: PV testers that measure V_oc and I_sc safely, insulation testers at the specified test voltage, clamp meters, irradiance meters. Measuring I_sc by shorting a string with improvised links is dangerous: breaking a DC short draws an arc.

**The laboratory.** The Commissioning tab gives the conditions and simulated "as-built" measurements; you enter your predictions and the standard's limit. Practice modes inject faults so you can recognise them; only a correctly predicted, healthy installation is recorded.`,diagrams:[{id:`string-config`,caption:`The DC side to be tested: strings, fuses, combiner and isolator.`}],formulas:[{name:`Expected string V_oc`,expr:`V_oc,str = n × [V_oc,STC(1 + β(T − 25)) + a·ln(G/1000)]`,where:[[`a`,`diode parameter of the module (given)`,`V`]]},{name:`Expected I_sc`,expr:`I_sc = I_sc,STC × G/1000 × (1 + α(T − 25))`,where:[]},{name:`Acceptance`,expr:`|measured − expected| ≤ tolerance × expected`,where:[]}],examples:[{title:`Expected V_oc and I_sc`,problem:`Strings of 2 illustrative 550 W modules (V_oc 49.6 V, I_sc 14.0 A, β −0.27 %/°C, α +0.048 %/°C, a = 2.6 V at 41 °C). Conditions: 850 W/m², module 41 °C.`,steps:[`V_oc per module = 49.6 × (1 − 0.0027 × 16) + 2.6 × ln 0.85 = 49.6 × 0.9568 − 0.42 = 47.46 − 0.42 = 47.04 V`,`String V_oc = 2 × 47.04 = 94.1 V`,`I_sc = 14.0 × 0.85 × (1 + 0.00048 × 16) = 11.9 × 1.0077 = 11.99 A`],answer:`Expect about 94 V (±5 %: 89–99 V) and 12.0 A (±10 %).`},{title:`Judging the measurements`,problem:`For those strings you measure: string 1 94.6 V, 12.1 A; string 2 −94.0 V, 11.8 A. Insulation resistance 40 MΩ; the standard’s limit for this voltage is 1 MΩ.`,steps:[`String 1 within tolerance; insulation passes`,`String 2: magnitude correct but negative: reversed polarity`],answer:`Do not connect string 2: correct its polarity, then re-test. Everything else passes.`}],applications:`Every new PV and battery installation is commissioned, and the record is kept for the owner, the installer and maintenance (course S5). Course S3 develops installation methods and the full test schedule.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Measuring V_oc without recording irradiance and temperature.`,correction:`Expected values depend on both; record them with every measurement.`},{mistake:`Shorting a string with a wire to “measure I_sc”.`,correction:`Use a PV tester designed for it; breaking a DC short draws an arc.`},{mistake:`Inventing an insulation-resistance limit.`,correction:`Take it from the applicable standard for the system voltage.`}],demo:{title:`Commissioning practice`,text:`On the Commissioning tab, predict V_oc and I_sc for the conditions, enter the standard’s insulation limit, and evaluate the as-built measurements; try the practice faults.`,link:e.solar(`Solar laboratory: commissioning`,`commission`),embed:`pv-iv`},activities:[e.solar(`Commissioning tab`,`commission`),e.proc(`PV open-circuit voltage test`,`solar-input`,`pv-voc`),e.proc(`Protective earth continuity`,`protective-earth`,`pe-continuity`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Expected I_sc: 13.8 A module at 750 W/m², 25 °C (A)?`,answer:10.35,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`13.8 × 0.75.`},{id:`k2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A string reads −120 V at the combiner. You…`,options:[`do not connect it; correct the polarity and re-test`,`connect it if the magnitude is right`,`insert the fuse to test it`,`ignore the sign`],answer:0,explain:`Reversed polarity.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`One string reads about one module’s voltage lower than the others. Likely cause?`,options:[`A short-circuited or bypassed module in that string`,`Low irradiance`,`A full battery`,`An RCD trip`],answer:0,explain:`One module’s voltage is missing.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The insulation-resistance pass limit comes from…`,options:[`the applicable standard for the system voltage`,`the module’s efficiency`,`the inverter’s colour`,`the installer’s preference`],answer:0,explain:`Standards set it.`}],summary:[`The design specifies DC and AC protection, isolation, earthing and labels.`,`Commissioning: inspect, test strings, check the battery, set, energise, test functions, record.`,`Predict V_oc and I_sc for the measured irradiance and temperature.`,`Deviations point to specific faults: reversed polarity, missing module, insulation damage.`,`The record supports maintenance and fault-finding for the system’s life.`],homework:{id:`S2.M5.L1.hw`,title:`Commissioning predictions`,brief:`Strings of 3 illustrative 400 W modules (V_oc 37.1 V, I_sc 13.8 A, β −0.27 %/°C, α +0.048 %/°C, a = 2.25 V at 37 °C). Conditions: 720 W/m², module 37 °C.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Module V_oc (V)?`,answer:35.16,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`37.1 × (1 − 0.0027 × 12) + 2.25 × ln 0.72 = 35.90 − 0.74 = 35.16 V.`},{id:`h2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`String V_oc (V)?`,answer:105.5,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`3 × 35.16.`},{id:`h3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`I_sc (A)?`,answer:9.993,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`13.8 × 0.72 × (1 + 0.00048 × 12) = 9.936 × 1.0058 = 9.99 A.`}],written:[{prompt:`List, in order, the commissioning steps you would carry out on this system before handing it over.`,model:`Visual inspection against the design; with isolators open: polarity, V_oc and I_sc of each string with irradiance and temperature recorded, insulation resistance against the standard’s limit; battery voltage and polarity; controller and inverter settings from datasheets; energise in the manufacturer’s order; functional tests (output, load, transfer, generator start, anti-islanding where applicable); complete and sign the record.`}]}}},{id:`S2.M5.L2`,title:`Solar system troubleshooting`,minutes:45,content:{objectives:[`Apply a systematic fault-finding method to solar and battery systems.`,`Interpret measurements to distinguish shading, soiling, open strings, reversed polarity, overvoltage, voltage drop, battery ageing, controller mismatch and setting errors.`,`Explain the safe way to take each measurement.`,`Recommend and verify a repair.`],introduction:`Solar systems usually fail gradually: a little less energy each month, an inverter that trips on cold mornings, lights that dim when a pump starts. Finding the cause needs a method, the right measurements and an understanding of what each one means. The laboratory's ten troubleshooting scenarios give you readings produced by the solar engine; this lesson teaches you to read them.`,lecture:`### The method

1. **Gather symptoms and history:** what changed, when, under what conditions? Check the monitoring data and the commissioning record.
2. **Make safe:** isolate as required (F1.M3); PV conductors stay live in daylight.
3. **Measure, comparing with expected values** for the conditions (lesson L1).
4. **Form a hypothesis** that explains *all* the readings, not just one.
5. **Confirm** with a targeted test.
6. **Repair**, then **verify** with the same measurements, and **record**.

### Reading the signs

| Symptom and readings | Likely cause |
|---|---|
| One string 0 A, others normal; full voltage across a fuse holder | Open string: blown fuse or open connector (find why it blew) |
| Stepped I–V curve with two peaks; low power | Partial shading; bypass diodes conducting |
| All currents low in proportion, V_oc normal | Soiling (or irradiance lower than thought) |
| Controller "PV overvoltage" on cold mornings | Strings too long for the coldest V_oc |
| Negative voltage at a combiner input | Reversed string polarity |
| Inverter low-voltage cut-out under load, battery terminals fine | Voltage drop in cable or connections |
| Large voltage dip on load steps, normal rested voltage | High battery internal resistance (ageing, a failing block) |
| Little charging with large modules on PWM | Controller–module mismatch |
| Loads shed though a generator is available | Generator start threshold below LVD |
| No power during a grid outage, grid-tied | Normal anti-islanding: not a fault |

### Measurements and their safety

- **Voltages** with a meter of the right category and DC rating; isolators open where testing string V_oc.
- **Currents** with a DC clamp meter, not by breaking live DC circuits.
- **Voltage drop under load:** measure simultaneously at both ends of a cable.
- **Internal resistance:** r = ΔV/ΔI from a known load step.
- **Insulation resistance:** with an insulation tester at the standard's test voltage, on isolated circuits only.

### Verify and record

After a repair, repeat the measurements that showed the fault and compare with expected values. Update the system record. A fuse that blew, an overvoltage or a hot joint each had a cause: find it, or the fault will return.`,technical:`**Monitoring data.** Modern controllers and inverters log PV voltage and current, battery voltage, SOC and events. Comparing daily yield with expected yield for the weather, and strings with each other, finds faults early: a string producing 50 % of its neighbour is never "weather".

**Thermal imaging.** An infrared camera shows hot cells (shading, cell cracks, failed bypass diodes) and hot joints (high resistance) at a distance, without contact. It complements electrical measurements.

**When to stop.** Damaged modules, melted connectors, battery swelling or a smell of electrolyte call for making the area safe and referring the work to a qualified person with the right equipment (F1.M4.L3).`,diagrams:[{id:`fault-method`,caption:`A systematic method: symptoms, make safe, measure, hypothesise, confirm, repair, verify, record.`}],formulas:[{name:`Internal resistance`,expr:`r = ΔV / ΔI`,where:[]},{name:`Cable and joint drop`,expr:`V_drop = V_battery − V_inverter = I × R_path`,where:[]},{name:`Uniform current loss`,expr:`loss = 1 − I_measured / I_expected`,where:[]}],examples:[{title:`Battery or cable?`,problem:`With the pump starting, the battery terminals read 24.1 V and the inverter terminals 20.3 V at 120 A. The rested battery voltage is 26.6 V. What do you conclude?`,steps:[`Path drop: 24.1 − 20.3 = 3.8 V at 120 A → R_path = 32 mΩ: far too high`,`Battery sag: 26.6 − 24.1 = 2.5 V at 120 A → r ≈ 21 mΩ: high too, but the cable path is the bigger problem`],answer:`Fix the cable and connections first (undersized cable or a poor joint), then re-test the battery’s internal resistance.`},{title:`Soiling`,problem:`Expected I_sc for the conditions is 12.4 A; all strings read 9.0–9.2 A; V_oc is within 2 % of expected; nothing is shaded.`,steps:[`Current loss = 1 − 9.1/12.4 = 27 %, uniform across strings`,`V_oc normal → cells working; less light reaching them`],answer:`Soiling: clean the modules and re-measure; add cleaning to the maintenance plan.`}],applications:`Fault-finding on solar and hybrid systems in the field, remote diagnosis from monitoring data, and the troubleshooting tab of the laboratory. Course S4 develops diagnostics and professional repair across the whole programme.`,mistakes:[{mistake:`Replacing a blown string fuse without finding why it blew.`,correction:`Test the string’s insulation and connectors first.`},{mistake:`Blaming the weather for one string’s low output.`,correction:`Compare strings: weather affects all of them alike.`},{mistake:`Measuring DC current by disconnecting a live conductor.`,correction:`Use a DC clamp meter; breaking DC under load draws an arc.`}],demo:{title:`Troubleshooting scenarios`,text:`Work through the laboratory’s ten scenarios: read the symptoms and engine-generated readings, choose a diagnosis, and study the explanation and fix.`,link:e.solar(`Solar laboratory: troubleshooting`,`faults`),embed:`mppt-track`},activities:[e.solar(`Troubleshooting tab`,`faults`),e.proc(`Battery terminal voltage-drop test`,`battery-terminals`,`terminal-drop`)],check:[{id:`k1`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`r from a 60 A step dropping 1.5 V (mΩ)?`,answer:25,tol:.01,unit:`mΩ`,explain:`1.5/60.`},{id:`k2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Uniform current loss: 8.2 A measured, 11.0 A expected (%)?`,answer:25.45,tol:.01,unit:`%`,explain:`1 − 0.745.`},{id:`k3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A controller reports PV overvoltage only on cold mornings. Cause?`,options:[`Strings too long for the cold V_oc`,`Soiling`,`Battery ageing`,`Shading`],answer:0,explain:`Voltage rises in the cold.`},{id:`k4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A hypothesis is accepted when it…`,options:[`explains all the readings and is confirmed by a test`,`explains the first reading`,`is the most common fault`,`is cheapest to fix`],answer:0,explain:`Systematic method.`}],summary:[`Method: symptoms → make safe → measure → hypothesise → confirm → repair → verify → record.`,`Compare measurements with expected values and with each other.`,`Patterns identify shading, soiling, open strings, polarity, overvoltage, drop, ageing, mismatch and settings.`,`Measure DC current with clamps; never break live DC.`,`Find the cause of every blown fuse or overvoltage.`],homework:{id:`S2.M5.L2.hw`,title:`Diagnose from readings`,brief:`Three parallel strings at the combiner: 11.9 A, 12.1 A, 0 A; array voltage 96 V; the third fuse holder reads 96 V across it.`,tasks:[{id:`h1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Diagnosis?`,options:[`The third string’s fuse is open`,`Shading on all strings`,`Battery failure`,`PV overvoltage`],answer:0,explain:`Full voltage across an open fuse; zero current.`},{id:`h2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Before replacing the fuse you should…`,options:[`isolate, prove dead and insulation-test that string to find why it blew`,`insert a larger fuse`,`bridge the fuse holder`,`ignore it`],answer:0,explain:`Find the cause.`}],written:[{prompt:`Describe how you would verify the repair.`,model:`With the string isolated, repeat polarity, V_oc and insulation resistance against expected values; insert the correctly rated fuse; close the isolator; measure the string current with a clamp meter and compare with the other strings under the same conditions; record the results.`}]}}}],quiz:{pass:.7,questions:[{id:`q1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The PV DC isolator must be rated for at least…`,options:[`the cold string V_oc and 1.25 × array I_sc`,`the battery voltage`,`the AC output voltage`,`the module efficiency`],answer:0,explain:`It must break the highest DC voltage on load.`},{id:`q2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Expected string V_oc: 4 modules × 36.0 V at the conditions (V)?`,answer:144,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`4 × 36.`},{id:`q3`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A measured string V_oc of 104 V against 108 V expected (±5 %) is…`,options:[`within tolerance`,`a fail`,`a reversed string`,`a short circuit`],answer:0,explain:`3.7 % low.`},{id:`q4`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Which must be recorded with every V_oc and I_sc measurement?`,options:[`Irradiance and module temperature`,`The weather forecast`,`The battery brand`,`The cable colour`],answer:0,explain:`Expected values depend on them.`},{id:`q5`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Path resistance: 2.4 V drop at 80 A (mΩ)?`,answer:30,tol:.01,unit:`mΩ`,explain:`2.4/80.`},{id:`q6`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Lights dim when the pump starts; rested voltage normal; r measured 5× its commissioning value. Cause?`,options:[`Battery ageing (high internal resistance)`,`Soiling`,`Anti-islanding`,`String fuse`],answer:0,explain:`High r.`},{id:`q7`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A grid-tied home has no power during an outage. This is…`,options:[`normal anti-islanding behaviour`,`an inverter failure`,`a blown string fuse`,`soiling`],answer:0,explain:`Not a fault.`},{id:`q8`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The safe way to measure a live PV string current is…`,options:[`a DC clamp meter`,`opening a connector under load`,`shorting it with a wire`,`touching it`],answer:0,explain:`No circuit is broken.`}]},assignment:{id:`S2.M5.asg`,title:`Module assignment: commissioning and fault report`,brief:`Commission your S2.M3 design in the solar laboratory, then solve at least five troubleshooting scenarios.`,tasks:[{id:`a1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`In the commissioning practice fault in which one string reads a negative voltage, the correct action is…`,options:[`leave it isolated, correct its polarity and re-test`,`insert its fuse`,`connect it and watch`,`replace the controller`],answer:0,explain:`Reversed polarity must be corrected before connection.`},{id:`a2`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`In the scenario where the inverter cuts out on pump start but the battery looks fine, the confirming measurement is…`,options:[`simultaneous voltages at the battery and inverter terminals under load`,`string V_oc`,`module temperature`,`insulation resistance`],answer:0,explain:`It reveals the path drop.`}],written:[{prompt:`Write a short commissioning record summary and a fault report for one scenario (symptom, readings, diagnosis, fix, verification).`,model:`Commissioning: conditions (G, T), expected and measured V_oc/I_sc per string within tolerance, insulation resistance above the standard’s limit, battery voltage, settings, functional tests passed. Fault report example: “cable-drop” — symptom inverter low-voltage trips on pump start; readings battery 25.4 V vs inverter 21.2 V at ~79 A; diagnosis excessive path resistance (6 mm² cable, poor joint); fix replace with the designed 16 mm² and re-terminate; verification drop now below 2 % under the same load.`}]},practicals:[{id:`S2.M5.P1`,title:`Solar laboratory: commission a design`,description:`Predict the expected values, evaluate the as-built measurements and pass the commissioning record for a healthy installation.`,link:e.solar(`Commissioning`,`commission`),evidence:{kind:`activity`,id:`solar:commission`}},{id:`S2.M5.P2`,title:`Troubleshooting: partial shading`,description:`Diagnose the shading scenario from its I–V and power readings.`,link:e.solar(`Troubleshooting`,`faults`),evidence:{kind:`activity`,id:`solar:fault:shading`}},{id:`S2.M5.P3`,title:`Troubleshooting: cold-morning overvoltage`,description:`Diagnose the controller-overvoltage scenario.`,link:e.solar(`Troubleshooting`,`faults`),evidence:{kind:`activity`,id:`solar:fault:voc-cold`}},{id:`S2.M5.P4`,title:`Troubleshooting: inverter cut-out on load`,description:`Diagnose the voltage-drop scenario.`,link:e.solar(`Troubleshooting`,`faults`),evidence:{kind:`activity`,id:`solar:fault:cable-drop`}},{id:`S2.M5.P5`,title:`Troubleshooting: generator does not start`,description:`Diagnose the generator-threshold scenario.`,link:e.solar(`Troubleshooting`,`faults`),evidence:{kind:`activity`,id:`solar:fault:gen-threshold`}},{id:`S2.M5.P6`,title:`Commissioning sequence drill`,description:`Put the commissioning steps for a PV-battery system in order, rejecting unsafe or wrong steps.`,task:{id:`s2-commission-sequence`,kind:`sequence`,title:`Commissioning a PV and battery system`,brief:`Choose the steps you would carry out, in order, to commission a new off-grid PV and battery system. Some offered steps are wrong or unsafe.`,steps:[`Inspect the installation visually against the design and labels`,`With the DC isolators open, check each string’s polarity`,`Measure each string’s V_oc and I_sc, recording irradiance and module temperature`,`Test the array insulation resistance against the standard’s limit`,`Check the battery bank voltage and polarity before connection`,`Set the controller and inverter from the battery datasheet`,`Energise in the manufacturer’s order and carry out functional tests`,`Complete and sign the commissioning record`],distractors:[{text:`Measure string I_sc by shorting the string with a wire and then pulling it off`,why:`Breaking a DC short draws an arc; use a PV tester designed for I_sc.`},{text:`Connect all strings first to see whether the controller reports a fault`,why:`A reversed or overvoltage string can destroy equipment; test every string before connection.`},{text:`Use generic charging voltages until the system has run for a week`,why:`Set-points must come from the battery datasheet from the start.`}],explain:`Inspect, test each string isolated (polarity, V_oc/I_sc with conditions, insulation), check the battery, configure from datasheets, energise in order with functional tests, and record.`},evidence:{kind:`task`,id:`s2-commission-sequence`}}]}],exam:{pass:.7,minutes:75,attempts:3,cooldownHours:24,questions:[{id:`e1`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A PV module behaves as a current source because…`,options:[`its current is set by irradiance over most of its voltage range`,`it stores charge`,`its voltage is fixed`,`it contains a battery`],answer:0,explain:`The flat part of the I–V curve.`},{id:`e2`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Cell temperature: NOCT 45 °C, ambient 30 °C, 800 W/m² (°C)?`,answer:55,tol:.01,unit:`°C`,explain:`30 + 25.`},{id:`e3`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`P_max of a 400 W module (γ −0.35 %/°C) at 55 °C, 1000 W/m² (W)?`,answer:358,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`400 × (1 − 0.105).`},{id:`e4`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Module V_oc (37.1 V, β −0.27 %/°C) at −5 °C (V)?`,answer:40.11,tol:.01,unit:`V`,explain:`37.1 × 1.081.`},{id:`e5`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Maximum in series under 150 V with V_oc,cold 40.11 V?`,answer:3,tol:.001,unit:``,explain:`3.74 → 3.`},{id:`e6`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Reverse current into a faulty string, 4 strings of I_sc 13.8 A (A)?`,answer:51.75,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`3 × 1.25 × 13.8.`},{id:`e7`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Minimum string fuse for I_sc 13.8 A (A)?`,answer:21.53,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`1.56 × 13.8.`},{id:`e8`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`With PWM on a 12 V battery, a 72-cell module operates…`,options:[`near battery voltage, far below its MPP`,`at its MPP`,`at V_oc`,`at zero current`],answer:0,explain:`PWM connects array to battery.`},{id:`e9`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`MPPT output current: 3 kW array, η 0.97, 48 V (A)?`,answer:60.63,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`2910/48.`},{id:`e10`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Grid-tied inverters stop during an outage because of…`,options:[`anti-islanding`,`battery protection`,`MPPT`,`overload`],answer:0,explain:`Protects line workers.`},{id:`e11`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Daily energy: fridge 150 W, duty 0.4, 24 h (Wh)?`,answer:1440,tol:.01,unit:`Wh`,explain:`150 × 0.4 × 24.`},{id:`e12`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Inverter for a 1.6 kW peak with 1.25 margin (W)?`,answer:2e3,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`1.25 × 1600.`},{id:`e13`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`P_array: E_d 4.5 kWh, η_bat 0.95, η_ctrl 0.97, PSH 5, k 0.8 (W)?`,answer:1221,tol:.01,unit:`W`,explain:`4500/3.686 = 1221 W.`},{id:`e14`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Battery energy: E_d 4.5 kWh, 2 days, DoD 0.8 (kWh)?`,answer:11.25,tol:.01,unit:`kWh`,explain:`4.5 × 2 / 0.8.`},{id:`e15`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Capacity of 11.25 kWh at 24 V (Ah)?`,answer:468.75,tol:.01,unit:`Ah`,explain:`11 250/24.`},{id:`e16`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Battery fuse for I_max 84 A (sizes 80/100/125 A) (A)?`,answer:125,tol:.001,unit:`A`,explain:`1.25 × 84 = 105 → 125 A.`},{id:`e17`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Generator for P_peak 1.8 kW and charger 3.2 kW (kVA)?`,answer:7.8125,tol:.01,unit:`kVA`,explain:`1.25 × 5000 / 0.8.`},{id:`e18`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`Lead-acid at 50 % DoD: valid generator thresholds?`,options:[`start 55 %, stop 90 %`,`start 30 %, stop 90 %`,`start 55 %, stop 50 %`,`start 10 %, stop 60 %`],answer:0,explain:`Start above the LVD (50 %), stop above start.`},{id:`e19`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Expected I_sc at 800 W/m², 25 °C, for 14.0 A STC (A)?`,answer:11.2,tol:.01,unit:`A`,explain:`14 × 0.8.`},{id:`e20`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`All strings read 25 % low in current with V_oc normal and no shade. Likely cause?`,options:[`Soiling`,`Reversed string`,`Cold overvoltage`,`Battery ageing`],answer:0,explain:`Uniform light loss.`},{id:`e21`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`A string reads a negative voltage during commissioning. You…`,options:[`keep it isolated and correct its polarity`,`insert the fuse`,`connect it to test`,`ignore it`],answer:0,explain:`Reversed polarity.`},{id:`e22`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Battery-to-inverter path resistance: 3 V drop at 100 A (mΩ)?`,answer:30,tol:.01,unit:`mΩ`,explain:`3/100.`},{id:`e23`,type:`numeric`,prompt:`Annual yield: 4 kWp, PSH 4.5, PR 0.8 (kWh)?`,answer:5256,tol:.01,unit:`kWh`,explain:`4 × 4.5 × 365 × 0.8.`},{id:`e24`,type:`multi`,prompt:`Which belong to an installation’s DC protection and isolation? (select all)`,options:[`String fuses where required`,`Lockable PV DC isolator`,`Battery fuse at the positive terminal`,`A wire link to short strings for testing`],answers:[0,1,2],explain:`Never improvise shorting links.`},{id:`e25`,type:`mcq`,prompt:`The insulation-resistance limit for commissioning comes from…`,options:[`the applicable standard for the system voltage`,`the controller manual only`,`a universal 10 kΩ`,`the installer’s choice`],answer:0,explain:`Standards set it.`}]}};export{t as S2};